{"id":17,"role":"Assistant","content":"The PDF is binary (can't extract text directly). Let me search for an HTML/text version and simultaneously read the key code structures to understand the formalization.\nTime: 2026-08-05 05:23:04 UTC","timestamp":1785907384,"token_count":45,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\",\"pattern\":\"transferMatrixPositivity_axiom\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"d86afd26-2465-4a57-8202-ffa1af638810","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"plaquetteBoltzmannPD\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/ReflectionPositivity.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"8fcb3f92-127a-47e0-a0cb-9984535c8692","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"query\":\"Osterwalder Schrader positivity Wilson action proof plaquette positive definite kernel lattice gauge\",\"num_results\":5}","name":"web_search"},"id":"072f40af-e77e-45ae-90ed-2b356b6a5f83","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":2185,"reasoning_content":"The PDF is binary and can't be read directly. Let me try to find the HTML version or a text version of the Osterwalder-Seiler proof. Let me search for the actual content of the proof. The key paper is \"General proof of Osterwalder-Schrader positivity for the Wilson action\" by Magnoli, Velo (1987). Let me look for an accessible version.\n\nActually, let me step back and think about the strategy here. The session 25 analysis concluded that:\n1. The L² expansion + reorganization approach has a deep obstacle (the reorganization step).\n2. The most promising alternative is the plaquette-by-plaquette induction approach.\n\nLet me think carefully about the mathematics here, because this is the crux of the whole project.\n\nThe goal is to prove reflection positivity for the transfer matrix. The key identity to close is `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom`, which says something like:\n`∫ ψ · Tψ dμ ≥ 0` (or the complex version `Complex.ofReal(∫ ψ · Tψ) = ⟨g, Tg⟩ ≥ 0`).\n\nLet me think about what the actual mathematical structure is. Let me re-read the key findings.\n\nFrom §8.11.30, the fullReflect form is:\n`Complex.ofReal(∫ ψ·Tψ dμ⁺⁰) = C · ∑_w F(w) · ∫_{u⁰} Ψ_w(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) · A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) dμ⁰`\n\nwhere:\n- C > 0 (from interface_boltzmann_character_expansion)\n- F(w) ≥ 0 (Fourier coefficients of the Boltzmann factor)\n- Ψ_w(u⁰) is the character factor\n- A_w(u⁰) is the \"amplitude\" — the product of interface link matrix elements\n- w* = fullReflectReindex dual w\n- σ is the reflection on the group (σ(g) = g⁻¹ for time-like, σ(g) = g for spatial)\n\nSo the integral to show ≥ 0 is:\n`∑_w F(w) · ∫ Ψ_w(u⁰) · A_w(u⁰) · A_{w*}(σ(u⁰)) dμ⁰`\n\nNow, the key insight from the analysis: this is a SINGLE integral over u⁰ (the interface link variables), not a double integral. The reflection positivity requires this single integral to be ≥ 0.\n\nLet me think about the plaquette-by-plaquette induction approach. \n\nActually, let me think more carefully about the structure. The standard Osterwalder-Seiler / Osterwalder-Schrader reflection positivity proof for lattice gauge theory works as follows:\n\nThe Wilson action is a sum over plaquettes: S = ∑_p S_p, where each S_p depends on the 4 link variables around the plaquette. The Boltzmann factor is exp(-β S) = ∏_p exp(-β S_p).\n\nReflection positivity says: for any function f of the links in the \"positive\" half-space (t ≥ 0), \n`⟨θ(f) · f⟩ ≥ 0`\nwhere θ is the reflection (t → -t), and ⟨·⟩ is the expectation with respect to the Gibbs measure.\n\nThe proof proceeds by:\n1. Each plaquette factor exp(-β S_p) is a positive-definite function of its link variables (this is the key lemma — for the Wilson action, exp(-β Re Tr(U_p)) is PD because it's a sum of characters with non-negative coefficients).\n2. The reflection maps plaquettes in the positive half-space to plaquettes in the negative half-space.\n3. The integral over all links can be done iteratively: first integrate over the links at the interface (t=0), then over the links at t=1, etc.\n\nActually, the standard proof is more subtle. Let me think about it in terms of the transfer matrix.\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on the Hilbert space L²(G^spatial) (functions of the spatial links at a fixed time). The reflection positivity is equivalent to T being a positive operator: ⟨f, Tf⟩ ≥ 0 for all f.\n\nThe transfer matrix is a product of \"single-time-slice\" transfer matrices, each corresponding to the Boltzmann weight of the plaquettes in a single time-slice. \n\nHmm, actually, let me think about this differently. The key structure is:\n\nThe full Boltzmann factor is a product over plaquettes. Each plaquette is either:\n- Entirely in the positive half-space (t > 0)\n- Entirely in the negative half-space (t < 0)  \n- Straddling the interface (t = 0 to t = 1, or t = -1 to t = 0)\n\nThe reflection θ maps positive plaquettes to negative plaquettes and vice versa. The interface plaquettes (straddling t=0) are mapped to themselves (or to their mirror).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\n`∫ f(U⁺) · f(θ(U⁺)) · ∏_p exp(-β S_p(U)) dU`\n\nwhere U⁺ are the links in the positive half-space and θ(U⁺) are the reflected links.\n\nNow, the plaquettes entirely in the positive half-space contribute factors that depend only on U⁺. The plaquettes entirely in the negative half-space contribute factors that depend only on U⁻ = θ(U⁺) (by reflection symmetry). The interface plaquettes depend on both U⁺ and U⁻ (specifically, on the interface links and the links just above/below).\n\nThe key idea of the induction: \n\nActually, I think the cleanest way to understand this is through the \"layer by layer\" or \"transfer matrix\" decomposition. Let me think about it as follows.\n\nConsider a finite lattice with time extent T. The links are:\n- Spatial links at each time slice t = 0, 1, ..., T-1 (in the positive half)\n- Temporal links connecting time slices\n\nThe Gibbs measure factorizes as a product of \"transfer matrices\":\n`Z = Tr(T^T)` (trace of T to the power T)\n\nwhere T is the transfer matrix. Reflection positivity is ⟨f, T^T f⟩ ≥ 0... no wait, that's not quite right either.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. Actually, the reflection positivity for the transfer matrix is:\n\nThe transfer matrix T is a positive semi-definite operator on L²(G^spatial). This means ⟨f, Tf⟩ ≥ 0 for all f.\n\nThe transfer matrix T for a single time step is:\n`(Tf)(U) = ∫ K(U, V) f(V) dV`\nwhere K(U, V) is the Boltzmann weight of the plaquettes in a single time slice, as a function of the spatial links U (at time t) and V (at time t+1).\n\nFor the Wilson action, K(U, V) = ∏_x exp(-β Re Tr(U_x V_x† W_x)) where W_x are the temporal links... hmm, this is getting complicated.\n\nActually, let me look at the actual structure in the codebase. Let me understand what `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` actually says and what the key lemmas are. Let me read the relevant parts of the code.\n\nActually, I realize I should focus on understanding the actual formalization structure rather than trying to reconstruct the physics from scratch. Let me read the key files.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` in ReflectionPositivity.lean\n2. The structure of the transfer matrix\n3. `plaquetteBoltzmannPD`\n4. The existing lemmas about the transfer matrix\n\nLet me also think about the plaquette-by-plaquette induction more carefully.\n\nThe key mathematical fact for the plaquette-by-plaquette approach:\n\n**Theorem (Reflection positivity, plaquette induction):** \nLet K₁, K₂, ..., Kₙ be PD kernels on a group G (or product of groups). Let θ be a reflection (involution). Then:\n`∫ f(g) · f(θ(g)) · ∏_i K_i(g_i) dg ≥ 0`\nfor all f, provided the K_i have a specific structure related to the reflection.\n\nHmm, but this isn't quite right. The issue is that the product of PD kernels is not necessarily \"reflection positive\" in the single-integral sense.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The key insight from the Osterwalder-Seiler proof is:\n\n**The reflection positivity integral can be written as a NORM SQUARED in an auxiliary Hilbert space.**\n\nSpecifically, for each plaquette factor exp(-β S_p), which is PD, we can write it as:\n`exp(-β S_p(U_p)) = ∑_λ a_λ |λ⟩⟨λ|(U_p)`\nwhere |λ⟩ are the representation matrices and a_λ ≥ 0.\n\nThe reflection positivity integral then becomes:\n`∫ f(U⁺) f(θU⁺) ∏_p [∑_λ a_λ ρ_λ(U_p)] dU`\n\nExpanding the product, we get a sum over assignments of representations to plaquettes. Each term is a product of representation matrices, integrated over the link variables. The integral over each link variable gives a \"contraction\" (Clebsch-Gordan coefficient).\n\nThe key point: after all the contractions, the result can be organized as ‖something‖² ≥ 0.\n\nThis is essentially the L² expansion approach, but done more carefully. The \"reorganization\" is the step where we organize the sum as a norm squared.\n\nHmm, so maybe the L² expansion IS the right approach, but the reorganization needs to be done more carefully.\n\nLet me think about the simplest case: a SINGLE time-like plaquette.\n\nA single time-like plaquette at position x has links:\n- U_x (spatial, at time 0, direction i)\n- U_{x+î} (temporal, at position x+î, time direction)  \n- U_x' (spatial, at time 1, direction i) — this is the reflected link\n- U_{x} (temporal, at position x, time direction)\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. Let me think about the lattice structure.\n\nActually, let me focus on what's already been proven and what the gap is. Let me read the code.\n\nLet me read the key parts of the codebase to understand the structure. I'll read:\n1. `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` and surrounding context in ReflectionPositivity.lean\n2. The transfer matrix definition\n3. `plaquetteBoltzmannPD`\n\nLet me be strategic about what I read given the context budget. Let me first grep for the key definitions.","turn":8,"is_prune_marker":false}
