{"id":24,"role":"Assistant","content":"","timestamp":1785907613,"token_count":0,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"d01aedc1-abf2-40d3-8447-85c028e2f3d2","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\",\"pattern\":\"def transferMatrix|transferMatrix :|noncomputable def transferMatrix\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"3be76a79-df48-49f9-99c9-1f16ea67cee7","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":1135,"reasoning_content":"Let me understand the structure better. The axiom `transferMatrixPositivity_axiom` says:\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0` where `G = osG` is the Osterwalder-Seiler Boltzmann factor.\n\nAnd `osG(U) = f(U) · exp(-β·S⁺(U)) · exp(-β·S_int(U)/2)`.\n\nSo `G(U)·G(θU) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S⁺(U))·exp(-β·S_int(U)/2)·exp(-β·S⁺(θU))·exp(-β·S_int(θU)/2)`.\n\nUsing `S⁺(θU) = S⁻(U)` and `S_int(θU) = S_int(U)`:\n`= f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·(S⁺(U) + S⁻(U) + S_int(U))) = f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U))`.\n\nSo the integral is `∫ f(U)·f(θU)·exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ₀ ≥ 0`.\n\nNow, the key question: how to prove this?\n\nLet me think about the Lüscher approach, which is mentioned in the search results. Lüscher constructs a transfer matrix explicitly and proves it's positive definite. The Osterwalder-Seiler approach uses the plaquette structure.\n\nLet me think about the structure of the proof more carefully. The key is the PRODUCT STRUCTURE of the Boltzmann factor.\n\n`exp(-β·S_W(U)) = ∏_p exp(-β·S_p(U_p))`\n\nwhere the product is over all plaquettes p, and S_p(U_p) is the action of a single plaquette (depending on the 4 links around it).\n\nEach plaquette factor `exp(-β·S_p(U_p)) = exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` (for the Wilson action, S_p = -Re Tr(U_p) where U_p is the plaquette variable = product of 4 links).\n\nWait, let me get the sign right. The Wilson action is `S_W = -∑_p Re Tr(U_p)` (so that the Boltzmann factor is `exp(β·∑_p Re Tr(U_p))`). Actually, conventions vary. Let me check.\n\nActually, the key point is: `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` is a PD function of the plaquette variable U_p ∈ SU(N). This is `plaquetteBoltzmannPD`.\n\nNow, the plaquette variable U_p is a product of 4 link variables. The links are either:\n- Spatial links (in the positive or negative half-space, or at the interface)\n- Temporal links\n\nThe reflection θ maps:\n- Positive half-space links → negative half-space links\n- Interface spatial links → interface spatial links (but possibly with dual/inverse)\n- Interface temporal links → interface temporal links (with dual/inverse)\n\nLet me think about the plaquette-by-plaquette induction more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest way to think about this is the Lüscher transfer matrix approach. Let me think about it.\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on L²(G^S) where S is the set of spatial sites (the spatial links at a fixed time slice). The transfer matrix for a single time step is:\n\n`(Tf)(U_spatial) = ∫ K(U_spatial, V_spatial) f(V_spatial) dV_spatial`\n\nwhere K is the Boltzmann weight of the plaquettes in a single time slice (the \"staples\").\n\nFor the Wilson action, the transfer matrix kernel is:\n`K(U, V) = ∏_x exp(β · Re Tr(U_x · W_x · V_x† · W'_x†))`\n\nwhere U_x, V_x are spatial links at consecutive times, and W_x, W'_x are the temporal links.\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about the structure differently.\n\nActually, the key insight for the Lüscher approach is:\n\nThe transfer matrix T can be written as T = T_spatial · T_temporal, where:\n- T_temporal is the contribution from the temporal plaquettes (which couple spatial links at consecutive times)\n- T_spatial is the contribution from the spatial plaquettes (which are within a single time slice)\n\nAnd both T_spatial and T_temporal are positive operators. T_spatial is positive because it's a multiplication operator by a positive function (the spatial plaquette Boltzmann factor). T_temporal is positive because of the PD property of the plaquette factor.\n\nWait, but the product of two positive operators is not necessarily positive (unless they commute). Hmm.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. In the Lüscher approach, the transfer matrix is constructed as follows:\n\nThe Hilbert space is L²(G^S) where S is the spatial lattice. The transfer matrix for one time step is:\n\n`T = exp(-a H_0) · exp(-a H_I)`\n\nwhere H_0 is the \"free\" part and H_I is the interaction. But this is for the Hamiltonian formulation.\n\nActually, I think I'm overcomplicating this. Let me go back to the concrete structure in the code and think about what's actually needed.\n\nLet me re-read the key identity. The axiom says:\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ ≥ 0`\n\nAnd we have the identity (proven): `∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰` (from `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg`).\n\nSo if we can prove T is a positive operator (i.e., `∫ g·Tg ≥ 0` for all g), we're done.\n\nNow, what is T? Let me look at the transfer matrix definition.\n\nLet me read the transfer matrix definition and the key identity.","turn":12,"is_prune_marker":false}
