{"id":30,"role":"Assistant","content":"Let me understand the key structural question: how the reflection acts on interface links, and whether the interface action splits cleanly.\nTime: 2026-08-05 05:35:44 UTC","timestamp":1785908144,"token_count":38,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\",\"pattern\":\"def positiveSites|def negativeSites|def interfaceSites\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"95d54644-cc01-4a85-b017-47ca407a5286","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"def reflectLinkVariable|def reflectToPosInterface\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"dd884dd4-80f5-4848-a988-01dbdc561de9","type":"function"},{"function":{"arguments":"{\"pattern\":\"def wilsonActionOSInterface|def wilsonActionOSPositive|def wilsonActionOSNegative\",\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\"}","name":"grep"},"id":"27e34068-74aa-42e1-ac77-6a330312521b","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":14755,"reasoning_content":"Now I understand the structure better. Let me think about the key mathematical structure.\n\nThe transfer matrix is:\n`(Tψ)(u) = ∫_{U⁻} ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u|_interface)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻`\n\nAnd the key identity is:\n`∫ G(U)·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g(u)·(Tg)(u) dμ⁺⁰`\n\nwhere `g(u) = f(u)·exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)`.\n\nSo to prove `∫ G·G(θU) ≥ 0`, we need `∫ g·Tg ≥ 0`, i.e., T is a positive operator.\n\nNow, the transfer matrix T has the form:\n`(Tψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u|_interface)) · K(u, U⁻) dμ⁻`\n\nwhere `K(u, U⁻) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u)))`.\n\nThe key observation: `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u|_interface)` maps the negative links U⁻ and the interface links u|_interface to a \"positive interface config\" — essentially, it reflects the negative links to positive links and keeps the interface links.\n\nSo T is an integral operator with kernel K. The question is whether T is posi