ransfer matrix** approach, where the transfer matrix is built up layer by layer, and each layer is a positive operator.\n\nLet me think about the Lüscher construction. The transfer matrix for a single time step is:\n\n`T = T_1 · T_2 · ... · T_n`\n\nwhere each T_k is associated with a single plaquette (or a single \"staple\"), and each T_k is a positive operator.\n\nThe key insight in Lüscher's construction is that each T_k is not just positive, but also has a specific form that allows the product to be positive.\n\nActually, I recall now. In Lüscher's construction, the transfer matrix is written as:\n\n`T = V · U`\n\nwhere:\n- V is a diagonal operator (multiplication by the spatial plaquette factor) — positive because it's multiplication by a positive function.\n- U is the \"hopping\" operator from the temporal plaquettes — positive because of the PD property.\n\nAnd the key is that V and U are BOTH positive, and the product V·U is positive because... hmm, actually, the product of two positive operators is not necessarily positive.\n\nWait, I think in Lüscher's case, V and U are both positive AND self-adjoint, and they're constructed so that the product is positive. Actually, I think the key is that U is not just positive but also has a specific kernel structure.\n\nLet me think about this more carefully. The hopping operator U has the form:\n`(Uf)(U_spatial) = ∫ K(U_spatial, V_spatial) f(V_spatial) dV_spatial`\n\nwhere K is the temporal plaquette factor. For a single temporal plaquette at site x:\n`K_x(U_x, V_x, W_x) = exp(β · Re Tr(U_x · W_x · V_x† · W'_x†))`\n\nwhere U_x, V_x are spatial links at consecutive times, and W_x, W'_x are temporal links.\n\nIf we fix the temporal links W_x, W'_x, then K_x is a function of U_x and V_x. The PD property of the plaquette factor means that K_x is a PD kernel in (U_x, V_x).\n\nFor a SINGLE temporal plaquette (single site x), the operator:\n`(U_x f)(U_x) = ∫ K_x(U_x, V_x) f(V_x) dV_x`\n\nis a positive operator (because K_x is