) = ∏_p exp(-β·S_p)` is a product of plaquette factors. Each plaquette factor is a function of 4 link variables.\n\nNow, the key idea: **integrate over the links in the \"temporal\" direction, one layer at a time.**\n\nConsider the links organized by time slice. At each time slice t, we have spatial links U_t (a vector of spatial links at all spatial sites). The temporal links connect consecutive time slices.\n\nThe Boltzmann factor can be written as:\n`exp(-β·S_W) = ∏_t K(U_t, U_{t+1}, W_t)`\n\nwhere K is the \"transfer matrix kernel\" for a single time step, depending on the spatial links at times t and t+1, and the temporal links W_t.\n\nWait, this isn't quite right because the spatial plaquettes (within a single time slice) don't couple different time slices. Let me separate:\n\n`exp(-β·S_W) = [∏_t exp(-β·S_spatial(U_t))] · [∏_t K_temporal(U_t, U_{t+1}, W_t)]`\n\nwhere S_spatial is the spatial plaquette action (within time slice t) and K_temporal is the temporal plaquette factor (coupling times t and t+1).\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral becomes:\n`I = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · [∏_t exp(-β·S_spatial(U_t))] · [∏_t K_temporal(U_t, U_{t+1}, W_t)] dμ`\n\nThe key insight: the spatial plaquette factors `exp(-β·S_spatial(U_t))` are POSITIVE functions (they're products of `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` which is positive). So they can be absorbed into f:\n\nLet `f̃(U) = f(U) · ∏_{t>0} exp(-β·S_spatial(U_t)/2)` (f with half the spatial plaquette factors).\n\nThen:\n`I = ∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · [∏_t K_temporal(U_t, U_{t+1}, W_t)] · [interface spatial factors] dμ`\n\nHmm, this is getting complicated. Let me think about it more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is simpler. Let me think about the transfer matrix for a SINGLE time step.\n\nThe transfer matrix for a single time step (from t to t+1) is:\n`(T_step φ)(U_t) = ∫ K(U_t, U_{t+1}) φ(U_{t+1}) dU_{t+1}`\n\nwhere K includes both the temporal plaquette factors and the spatial plaquette factors at time t+1:\n`K(U_t, U_{t+1}) = exp(-β·S_spatial(U_{t+1})) · ∏_x K_temporal_x(U_t(x), U_{t+1}(x), W_t(x))`\n\nNow, the key: K is a PD kernel (in U_t, U_{t+1}) because:\n1. The spatial plaquette factor `exp(-β·S_spatial(U_{t+1}))` is a positive function of U_{t+1}.\n2. The temporal plaquette factor `∏_x K_temporal_x` is a PD kernel (tensor product of PD kernels).\n\nAnd the product of a positive function and a PD kernel is a PD kernel (this is the Schur product theorem for kernels, or can be seen from the integral).\n\nWait, is that true? If K₁(u, v) is PD and g(v) is positive, is K₂(u, v) = K₁(u, v)·g(v) PD?\n\n`∫∫ K₂(u, v) f(u) f(v) du dv = ∫∫ K₁(u, v) f(u) g(v)^{1/2} g(v)^{1/2} f(v) du dv`\n`= ∫∫ K₁(u, v) [f(u) g(u)^{1/2}] [g(v)^{1/2} f(v)] · [g(u)^{-1/2} g(v)^{1/2}] du dv`\n\nHmm, that doesn't work directly. Let me think again.\n\nActually, if K₁(u, v) is PD and g is positive, then K₂(u, v) = g(u)^{1/2} · K₁(u, v) · g(v)^{1/2} is PD (this is the \"congruence\" property). But K₂(u, v) = K₁(u, v) · g(v) is NOT necessarily PD.\n\nHowever, K₂(u, v) = K₁(u, v) · g(v) gives:\n`∫∫ K₂(u, v) f(u) f(v) du dv = ∫∫ K₁(u, v) f(u) [g(v) f(v)] du dv`\n\nThis is `⟨f, T₁(g·f)⟩` where T₁ is the operator with kernel K₁. This is NOT necessarily non-negative.\n\nBut if K₁ is PD, then T₁ is positive, so `⟨f, T₁(g·f)⟩ ≥ 0` iff... hmm, no, `⟨f, T₁h⟩` is not necessarily non-negative for arbitrary h.\n\nOK so the product of a PD kernel and a positive function is NOT necessarily PD. This means the transfer matrix kernel K (which is the product of a PD temporal kernel and a positive spatial function) is NOT necessarily PD.\n\nSo the single-step transfer matrix is NOT necessarily a positive operator. This means the Lüscher approach must use a different argument.\n\nLet me reconsider. I think the key in Lüscher's approach is:\n\nThe transfer matrix is T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2}, where:\n- V is the spatial plaquette factor (multiplication operator, positive)\n- U is the temporal plaquette operator (PD kernel, positive)\n\nThen T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2} is positive because U is positive (and V^{1/2} is self-adjoint).\n\nAnd `⟨f, Tf⟩ = ⟨V^{1/2}f, U(V^{1/2}f)⟩ ≥ 0` because U is positive.\n\nSo the reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ⟨f, Tf⟩ = ⟨f, V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}·f⟩ = ⟨V^{1/2}f, U·V^{1/2}·f⟩ ≥ 0`\n\nThis works because:\n1. V is a positive multiplication operator (spatial plaquette factor is positive).\n2. U is a positive integral operator (temporal plaquette factor is PD).\n3. T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} is positive (sandwich of positive U between V^{1/2}).\n\nThis is the key insight! The transfer matrix is NOT V·U but V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}, and this IS positive.\n\nNow, how does this relate to the reflection positivity integral?\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ`\n\nThe Boltzmann factor decomposes as:\n`exp(-β·S_W) = exp(-β·S_spatial) · exp(-β·S_temporal)`\n\nwhere S_spatial is the sum of spatial plaquettes and S_temporal is the sum of temporal plaquettes.\n\nNow, `exp(-β·S_spatial)` is a positive function. And `exp(-β·S_temporal)` is a PD kernel (in the appropriate variables).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral becomes:\n`I = ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β·S_spatial(U)) · exp(-β·S_temporal(U)) dμ`\n\nNow, the spatial plaquette factor `exp(-β·S_spatial(U))` can be split between the positive and negative halves:\n`exp(-β·S_spatial(U)) = exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁻(U⁻))`\n\nwhere S_spatial⁺ is the spatial plaquettes in the positive half and S_spatial⁻ is those in the negative half. By reflection symmetry, `S_spatial⁻(U⁻) = S_spatial⁺(reflect(U⁻))`.\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(reflect(U⁻))) · exp(-β·S_temporal(U)) dμ`\n\nLet `g(U⁺, u⁰) = f(U⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺, u⁰)/2)` (f with half the spatial factor).\n\nWait, but the spatial plaquettes at the interface might involve interface links. Let me not worry about that for now.\n\nThen:\n`I = ∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · exp(-β·S_temporal(U)) dμ`\n\nwhere I've absorbed the spatial factors into g (using the fact that `exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(reflect(U⁻))) = [exp(-β·S_spatial⁺/2)]²` and splitting between the two f's).\n\nNow, `exp(-β·S_temporal(U))` is the temporal plaquette factor, which is a PD kernel. The integral becomes:\n`I = ∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰) dμ`\n\nwhere K_temporal is the temporal plaquette factor.\n\nNow, the temporal plaquette factor is a product over sites x of `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p(x)))` where U_p(x) is the temporal plaquette at x. Each temporal plaquette involves:\n- A spatial link at time t (U_t(x))\n- A temporal link at time t (W_t(x))\n- A spatial link at time t+1 (U_{t+1}(x))\n- A temporal link at time t+1 (W_{t+1}(x))... \n\nHmm wait, actually a temporal plaquette in the (0, i) plane at site (t, x) involves:\n- U((t,x), 0): temporal link\n- U((t+1,x), i): spatial link at t+1\n- U((t,x)+î, 0)†: temporal link (inverted)\n- U((t,x), i)†: spatial link at t (inverted)\n\nSo the temporal plaquette at (t, x) couples the spatial links at times t and t+1 (at sites x and x+î) and the temporal links at time t (at sites x and x+î).\n\nFor the reflection positivity, the temporal plaquettes at the interface (t=0) couple:\n- Spatial links at t=0 (interface) and t=1 (positive)\n- Temporal links at t=0 (interface or positive?)\n\nAnd the temporal plaquettes at t=-1 couple:\n- Spatial links at t=-1 (negative) and t=0 (interface)\n- Temporal links at t=-1 (negative)\n\nBy reflection, the t=-1 plaquettes are the mirror of the t=0 plaquettes.\n\nOK, I think the key insight is:\n\n**The temporal plaquette factor is a PD kernel in the spatial link variables (at consecutive times), with the temporal links as parameters.**\n\nSpecifically, for fixed temporal links W, the temporal plaquette factor:\n`K_temporal(U_t, U_{t+1}; W) = ∏_x exp(β·Re Tr(U_t(x) · W(x) · U_{t+1}(x)† · W(x+î)†))`\n\nis a PD kernel in (U_t, U_{t+1}) — this is because each factor `exp(β·Re Tr(A·W·B†·W'†))` is PD in (A, B) (it's the plaquette Boltzmann factor, which is PD by `plaquetteBoltzmannPD`), and the product (tensor product over sites x) of PD kernels is PD.\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral (after absorbing the spatial factors) is:\n`I = ∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ`\n\nwhere W are the temporal links.\n\nThe temporal plaquette factor K_temporal couples:\n- Positive spatial links U⁺ (at t=1, 2, ...)\n- Negative spatial links U⁻ (at t=-1, -2, ...)\n- Interface spatial links u⁰ (at t=0)\n- Temporal links W (at all times)\n\nThe key: the temporal plaquette factor can be written as a product of \"single-time-step\" kernels:\n`K_temporal = ∏_t K_step(U_t, U_{t+1}; W_t)`\n\nwhere K_step is the kernel for a single time step.\n\nFor the reflection positivity, the integral over the positive links U⁺ and negative links U⁻ can be done using the PD property of K_step.\n\nActually, I think the cleanest way to see this is:\n\n**The reflection positivity integral is a \"contraction\" of a PD kernel, which gives a PD kernel (and hence a non-negative integral).**\n\nSpecifically, the temporal plaquette factor K_temporal is a PD kernel in ALL the spatial link variables (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰). The reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰`\n\nNow, if K_temporal is PD in (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰), then:\n`I = ∫∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰`\n\nThis is NOT the standard PD quadratic form `∫∫ f(x)·conj(f(y))·K(x,y) dx dy` because:\n- The first g is evaluated at (U⁺, u⁰)\n- The second g is evaluated at (reflect(U⁻), u⁰*), NOT at (U⁺, u⁰)\n\nSo the two g's are evaluated at DIFFERENT points, and the kernel is evaluated at (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰), NOT at the \"difference\" of the two points.\n\nThis is exactly the obstruction identified in §8.11.31: the single integral `∫ f(g)·f(g⁻¹)·K(g) dg` is NOT the standard PD quadratic form.\n\nHmm, so even with the Lüscher decomposition, we still face the same obstruction?\n\nWait, let me think about this more carefully. The Lüscher approach gives T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} which is positive. The reflection positivity is `⟨f, Tf⟩ ≥ 0`. But this is the INNER PRODUCT `⟨f, Tf⟩ = ∫ f(u)·(Tf)(u) du`, which IS the standard PD quadratic form (T is a positive operator, so ⟨f, Tf⟩ ≥ 0).\n\nSo the question is: does the Lüscher decomposition T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} apply to OUR transfer matrix?\n\nLet me check. Our transfer matrix is:\n`(Tψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻`\n\nThis is NOT of the form `(Tψ)(u) = ∫ K(u, v) ψ(v) dv` because the argument of ψ is `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)` which depends on BOTH U⁻ and u (specifically u⁰).\n\nHmm, so our transfer matrix is not a standard integral operator. Let me think about why.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that our \"transfer matrix\" is defined differently from the Lüscher transfer matrix. In the Lüscher approach, the transfer matrix acts on functions of the SPATIAL links at a SINGLE time slice, and it propagates from one time slice to the next. In our formulation, the transfer matrix acts on functions of ALL positive + interface links, and it \"integrates out\" the negative links.\n\nThese are different objects. The Lüscher transfer matrix is a single-step operator, while ours is a \"half-space\" operator.\n\nLet me think about how they're related. The Lüscher transfer matrix T_step acts on L²(G^S) (functions of spatial links at one time slice). The full transfer matrix (for the entire positive half-space) is:\n`T_full = T_step^{T/2}` (the T/2-th power of the single-step transfer matrix, where T is the time extent).\n\nAnd the reflection positivity is `⟨f, T_full f⟩ ≥ 0`, which follows from T_step being positive (because a positive operator raised to a positive power is positive).\n\nWait, is that true? If T_step is positive, is T_step^n positive? Yes, because T_step^n = T_step^{n/2}·T_step^{n/2} (for even n), and T_step^{n/2} is self-adjoint (if T_step is self-adjoint), so T_step^n = (T_step^{n/2})†·T_step^{n/2} is positive.\n\nActually, T_step^n is positive if T_step is positive and self-adjoint, because T_step^n = (T_step^{n/2})†·T_step^{n/2} (using the spectral theorem). For odd n, T_step^n = T_step·(T_step^{(n-1)/2})†·T_step^{(n-1)/2} = T_step^{1/2}·(T_step^{(n-1)/2})†·T_step^{(n-1)/2}·T_step^{1/2}... hmm, this is getting complicated. Actually, for a positive self-adjoint operator A, A^n is positive for all n ≥ 0 (by the spectral theorem, since the spectrum of A is in [0, ∞), the spectrum of A^n is also in [0, ∞)).\n\nSo if T_step is positive and self-adjoint, then T_full = T_step^{T/2} is positive, and `⟨f, T_full f⟩ ≥ 0`.\n\nBut wait, is T_step self-adjoint? In the Lüscher construction, T_step = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} where V is self-adjoint (multiplication by positive function) and U is self-adjoint (PD kernel with real symmetric kernel). The product V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} is self-adjoint (because (V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2})† = V^{1/2}·U†·V^{1/2} = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}). So yes, T_step is self-adjoint and positive.\n\nSo the Lüscher approach gives:\n1. T_step = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} is positive and self-adjoint.\n2. T_full = T_step^{T/2} is positive.\n3. `⟨f, T_full f⟩ ≥ 0` gives reflection positivity.\n\nNow, the question is: can we formalize this in Lean?\n\nThe key steps would be:\n1. Define the single-step transfer matrix T_step.\n2. Show T_step = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} where V is the spatial plaquette factor and U is the temporal plaquette operator.\n3. Show U is positive (using `plaquetteBoltzmannPD`).\n4. Show V is positive (multiplication by positive function).\n5. Conclude T_step is positive.\n6. Show T_full = T_step^{T/2} (or the appropriate power).\n7. Show `⟨f, T_full f⟩ ≥ 0`.\n\nBut this requires a significant restructuring of the transfer matrix. The current transfer matrix `transferMatrixCorrect` is the \"half-space\" transfer matrix, not the single-step one.\n\nHmm, but actually, maybe we don't need to restructure. Let me think about whether the current transfer matrix can be directly shown to be positive using the Lüscher idea.\n\nThe current transfer matrix is:\n`(Tψ)(u) = ∫ ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻`\n\nThe key: `exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u)))` is the Boltzmann factor for the \"interface\" region, split between positive and negative halves.\n\nNow, `S⁺(u)` depends only on u (positive + interface links), so `exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)` is a multiplication operator. And `S⁻(U⁻,u)` depends on U⁻ and u⁰ (not u⁺), so `exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻,u)/2)` is a function of U⁻ and u⁰.\n\nLet me write:\n`(Tψ)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ ψ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nNow, `ψ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰)` — the function ψ evaluated at the config with positive links = reflect(U⁻) and interface links = u⁰. If ψ = g_posInterface, then:\n`g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(reflect(U⁻), u⁰)/2) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2)`\n\nSo:\n`(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n`= exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nAnd:\n`g(u)·(Tg)(u) = f(u) · exp(-β·S⁺(u)) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nNow, the key observation: the inner integral (over U⁻) does NOT depend on u⁺ (the positive links), only on u⁰ (the interface links). So:\n\n`∫ g·Tg dμ⁺⁰ = ∫ [∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺] · [∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻] dμ⁰`\n\nLet me define:\n`A(u⁰) = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nThen `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰)·B(u⁰) dμ⁰`.\n\nNow, by the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺) (measure-preserving):\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) + S_int(reflect(V⁺), u⁰))) dμ⁺`\n\nBy reflection symmetry:\n- `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S⁺(V⁺, u⁰*)` (where u⁰* is the reflected interface links)\n- `S_int(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S_int(V⁺, u⁰*)` (interface action is reflection-symmetric)\n\nWait, but `S_int(reflect(V⁺), u⁰)` — the interface action at the config with negative links = reflect(V⁺) and interface links = u⁰. By the reflection symmetry `S_int(θU) = S_int(U)`, we have `S_int(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S_int(θ(reflect(V⁺), u⁰))`. And `θ(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = (V⁺, ..., u⁰*)`. So `S_int(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S_int(V⁺, u⁰*)`.\n\nBut wait, `S_int(V⁺, u⁰*)` is the interface action at a config with positive links V⁺ and interface links u⁰*. This depends on V⁺ and u⁰*.\n\nHmm, but I want to relate B(u⁰) to A(u⁰*). Let me check:\n`A(u⁰*) = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰*) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰*)) dμ⁺`\n\nAnd:\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, u⁰*) + S_int(V⁺, u⁰*))) dμ⁺`\n\nSo `B(u⁰) ≠ A(u⁰*)` in general, because B has the extra `S_int` factor.\n\nHmm. So the interface action complicates things. Let me think about how to handle the interface action.\n\nThe interface action S_int consists of plaquettes straddling the t=0 plane. These involve:\n- Positive links (at t=1)\n- Negative links (at t=-1)\n- Interface links (at t=0)\n\nThe interface plaquettes are of two types:\n1. \"Upper\" interface plaquettes: involving positive links (t=1) and interface links (t=0). These are part of S⁺ or S_int?\n2. \"Lower\" interface plaquettes: involving negative links (t=-1) and interface links (t=0). These are part of S⁻ or S_int?\n\nI think the decomposition is:\n- S⁺ includes the upper interface plaquettes (positive + interface)\n- S⁻ includes the lower interface plaquettes (negative + interface)\n- S_int is the \"correction\" for the plaquettes that involve BOTH positive and negative links (if any)\n\nActually, wait. For a 2D lattice (1 time + 1 space), a time-space plaquette at (t=0, x) involves:\n- U((0,x), 0): temporal link at t=0 (interface)\n- U((1,x), 1): spatial link at t=1 (positive)\n- U((0,x+1), 0)†: temporal link at t=0 (interface)\n- U((0,x), 1)†: spatial link at t=0 (interface)\n\nSo this plaquette involves positive and interface links, but NOT negative links. It's an \"upper\" interface plaquette.\n\nA time-space plaquette at (t=-1, x) involves:\n- U((-1,x), 0): temporal link at t=-1 (negative)\n- U((0,x), 1): spatial link at t=0 (interface)\n- U((-1,x+1), 0)†: temporal link at t=-1 (negative)\n- U((-1,x), 1)†: spatial link at t=-1 (negative)\n\nSo this plaquette involves negative and interface links, but NOT positive links. It's a \"lower\" interface plaquette.\n\nSo the interface plaquettes DON'T involve both positive and negative links! Each interface plaquette involves either (positive + interface) or (negative + interface), but not both.\n\nThis means:\n- S⁺ = positive plaquettes (all positive links) + upper interface plaquettes (positive + interface links)\n- S⁻ = negative plaquettes (all negative links) + lower interface plaquettes (negative + interface links)\n- S_int = ??? \n\nWait, but the code has a separate S_int. Let me re-examine.\n\nActually, I think the decomposition might be:\n- S⁺ = plaquettes entirely in the positive half (t ≥ 1)\n- S⁻ = plaquettes entirely in the negative half (t ≤ -1)\n- S_int = plaquettes straddling the interface (involving links at t=0 and t=±1)\n\nAnd the \"straddling\" plaquettes are the time-space plaquettes at t=0 (upper) and t=-1 (lower). These involve:\n- Upper: temporal links at t=0 (interface), spatial links at t=1 (positive), spatial links at t=0 (interface)\n- Lower: temporal links at t=-1 (negative), spatial links at t=0 (interface), spatial links at t=-1 (negative)\n\nSo the upper interface plaquettes involve positive and interface links, and the lower ones involve negative and interface links.\n\nNow, the key: the interface action S_int = S_int_upper + S_int_lower, where:\n- S_int_upper depends on positive and interface links\n- S_int_lower depends on negative and interface links\n\nAnd by reflection, S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰) = S_int_upper(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*).\n\nNow, going back to the transfer matrix:\n`(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nwhere `S_int(U⁻, u⁰) = S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int_upper(∅, u⁰)`... \n\nHmm wait, does S_int_upper depend on positive links? If so, then S_int(U⁻, u⁰) would depend on u⁺ too, which contradicts my earlier claim that the inner integral doesn't depend on u⁺.\n\nLet me re-examine. The transfer matrix formula has `S_int(U⁻, u)` where u = (u⁺, u⁰). If S_int depends on u⁺, then the inner integral over U⁻ would depend on u⁺, and we couldn't separate the integrals.\n\nBut the code says `wilsonActionOSInterface N T L β (extendToFullConfig N T L U_minus u)` — this is the interface action at the full config (U⁻, u⁺, u⁰). If the interface action depends on u⁺, then the inner integral depends on u⁺.\n\nHmm, but the key identity `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` was PROVEN, so the factorization must work out. Let me re-examine.\n\nActually, looking at the transfer matrix definition more carefully:\n```\ntransferMatrixCorrect (ψ) (u) = ∫ ψ(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u|_interface)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻\n```\n\nwhere `u` is a PosInterfaceConfig (positive + interface links), and `extendToFullConfig N T L U_minus u` creates the full config from U⁻ and u.\n\nThe interface action `S_int(extendToFullConfig(U⁻, u))` depends on the full config, which includes u⁺. So S_int DOES depend on u⁺.\n\nBut then, the inner integral over U⁻ depends on u⁺ (through S_int), and we can't separate the integrals as I did above.\n\nWait, but the identity `integral_G_thetaG_eq_inner_g_Tg` was proven. Let me re-read the proof to see how the factorization works.\n\nActually, looking at the proof (lines 4857-4865), the key identity is:\n```\n∫ (Uneg) H(e(rho.symm(x, Uneg))) dmuN = g(m) * transferMatrixCorrect(g)(m)\n```\nwhere `m = mergePosInterface(x.1, x.2)` and `x = (U_pos, u_int)`.\n\nSo the integral over U⁻ (negative links) gives `g(m) * T(g)(m)` where m is the merged positive+interface config. This is then integrated over (U_pos, u_int) to give `∫ g·Tg`.\n\nSo the structure is:\n`∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫_{(U⁺, u⁰)} [∫_{U⁻} H(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰) dμ⁻] dμ⁺ dμ⁰ = ∫_{(U⁺, u⁰)} g(U⁺, u⁰) · T(g)(U⁺, u⁰) dμ⁺ dμ⁰`\n\nwhere H is the integrand G(U)·G(θU).\n\nSo the inner integral over U⁻ gives `g(u)·T(g)(u)` where u = (U⁺, u⁰). And T(g)(u) involves an integral over U⁻ that DOES depend on u⁺ (through S_int).\n\nSo the factorization I did earlier (separating A and B) was WRONG because S_int depends on u⁺.\n\nOK so let me reconsider. The transfer matrix T is:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∫ g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u))) dμ⁻`\n\nwhere `S_int(U⁻, u)` depends on U⁻, u⁺, and u⁰.\n\nAnd `g(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2)`.\n\nSo:\n`g(u⁺, u⁰) · T(g)(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)) · ∫ g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u))) dμ⁻`\n\nNow, `g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(reflect(U⁻), u⁰)/2) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2)`.\n\nSo:\n`g·Tg = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u))) dμ⁻`\n\n`= f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S⁻(U⁻, u)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u)/2)) dμ⁻`\n\nHmm wait, I'm getting confused with the factors. Let me be very careful.\n\n`S⁻(U⁻, u)` in the transfer matrix formula — this is `wilsonActionOSNegative(extendToFullConfig(U⁻, u))`. The negative action depends on the negative links and the interface links (NOT the positive links), because the negative plaquettes are entirely in the negative half. So `S⁻(U⁻, u) = S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)` (independent of u⁺).\n\nBut `S_int(U⁻, u)` = `wilsonActionOSInterface(extendToFullConfig(U⁻, u))` — the interface action. This DOES depend on u⁺ (because the upper interface plaquettes involve positive links).\n\nSo the transfer matrix is:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2 + S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\n`= exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nAnd the interface action `S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰)` depends on all three: U⁻, u⁺, u⁰.\n\nNow, the interface action consists of:\n- Upper interface plaquettes: depend on u⁺ and u⁰ (positive + interface links)\n- Lower interface plaquettes: depend on U⁻ and u⁰ (negative + interface links)\n\nSo `S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰) = S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰) + S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰)`.\n\nAnd by reflection, `S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰) = S_int_upper(reflect(U⁻), u⁰*)`.\n\nSo:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰)) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nNow the inner integral over U⁻ does NOT depend on u⁺! It only depends on u⁰. So:\n\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2 + S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\nwhere `B(u⁰) = ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int_lower(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`.\n\nAnd:\n`g(u⁺, u⁰) · T(g)(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2 + S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\n`= f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰) + S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\nNow, `S⁺(u⁺, u⁰) + S_int_upper(u⁺, u⁰)` is the action of all plaquettes involving positive links (both the purely positive plaquettes and the upper interface plaquettes). Let me call this `S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)`.\n\nSo:\n`g·Tg = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) · B(u⁰)`\n\nAnd:\n`∫ g·Tg dμ⁺⁰ = ∫ [∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺] · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nLet `A(u⁰) = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`.\n\nThen `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`.\n\nNow, by the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺):\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) + S_int_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰))) dμ⁺`\n\nBy reflection symmetry:\n- `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S⁺(V⁺, u⁰*)` (negative action at reflected config = positive action at original)\n- `S_int_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S_int_upper(V⁺, u⁰*)` (lower interface at reflected = upper interface at original with reflected interface links)\n\nSo:\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, u⁰*) + S_int_upper(V⁺, u⁰*))) dμ⁺`\n\nHmm, but this has `u⁰` in f and `u⁰*` in the exponential. If `u⁰* = u⁰` (interface links are fixed by reflection), then:\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺ = A(u⁰)`\n\nAnd then `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰)² dμ⁰ ≥ 0`!\n\nBut if `u⁰* ≠ u⁰` (interface links are modified by reflection), then `B(u⁰) = A(u⁰*)`... wait, no. Let me be more careful.\n\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, u⁰*)) dμ⁺`\n\nThis is NOT `A(u⁰)` because the exponential has `u⁰*` while f has `u⁰`. And it's NOT `A(u⁰*)` because f has `u⁰` while A(u⁰*) would have f(V⁺, u⁰*).\n\nSo the question is: **are the interface links fixed by the reflection (u⁰* = u⁰), or are they modified?**\n\nThis is crucial. If the interface links are fixed by reflection, then B(u⁰) = A(u⁰) and `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A² ≥ 0`. If not, we need a more subtle argument.\n\nLet me check what the reflection does to the interface links. The interface links are at t=0. The reflection θ maps t → -t. So t=0 maps to t=0 (fixed). But the DIRECTION of the links might change.\n\nFor spatial links at t=0: U((0,x), i) → U((0,x), i) (fixed, because the site (0,x) maps to (0,x) and the direction i is spatial, unchanged by time reflection).\n\nFor temporal links at t=0: U((0,x), 0) → U((-1,x), 0)† or U((0,x), 0) → U((0,x), 0)†? \n\nThe temporal link U((0,x), 0) goes from (0,x) to (1,x). Under reflection t → -t, this maps to the link from (0,x) to (-1,x), which is U((-1,x), 0)† (the inverse of the link from (-1,x) to (0,x)).\n\nWait, actually, the temporal link at (0,x) in direction 0 goes from site (0,x) to site (1,x). Under reflection, site (0,x) → (0,x) and site (1,x) → (-1,x). So the link from (0,x) to (1,x) maps to the link from (0,x) to (-1,x), which is U((-1,x), 0)⁻¹ (the inverse of the link from (-1,x) to (0,x)).\n\nSo the temporal interface link U((0,x), 0) maps to U((-1,x), 0)⁻¹, which is a NEGATIVE link (at t=-1), not an interface link!\n\nHmm, so the temporal links at t=0 are NOT interface links — they're mapped to negative links by the reflection. Let me reconsider the classification.\n\nActually, I think the issue is about how the \"interface\" is defined. Let me look at the code to understand the classification of sites into positive, negative, and interface.\n\nLet me check the definitions of `positiveSites`, `negativeSites`, `interfaceSites`.\n\nActually, I think the key question is: what is the reflection plane? If the reflection plane is at t=0 (a site plane), then:\n- Sites at t > 0 are positive\n- Sites at t < 0 are negative\n- Sites at t = 0 are interface\n\nAnd the links are classified by their starting site:\n- Links starting at t > 0: positive\n- Links starting at t < 0: negative\n- Links starting at t = 0: interface\n\nUnder this classification:\n- Spatial links at t=0: interface (start at t=0, direction spatial)\n- Temporal links at t=0: interface (start at t=0, direction temporal) — these go from t=0 to t=1\n\nThe reflection maps:\n- Spatial link at (0,x) direction i → spatial link at (0,x) direction i (fixed, because (0,x) → (0,x) and i is spatial)\n- Temporal link at (0,x) direction 0 → ??? \n\nThe temporal link at (0,x) goes from (0,x) to (1,x). Under reflection, (0,x) → (0,x) and (1,x) → (-1,x). So the link from (0,x) to (1,x) maps to the link from (0,x) to (-1,x). But the link from (0,x) to (-1,x) is the INVERSE of the link from (-1,x) to (0,x), which is U((-1,x), 0)⁻¹.\n\nSo the temporal interface link U((0,x), 0) maps to U((-1,x), 0)⁻¹, which is a NEGATIVE link (starting at t=-1).\n\nThis means the temporal interface links are NOT fixed by the reflection — they're mapped to negative links (with inversion).\n\nHmm, but then the \"interface\" only contains spatial links at t=0, and the temporal links at t=0 are... well, they start at t=0 so they're interface by the classification, but they map to negative links under reflection.\n\nThis is getting complicated. Let me look at the actual code to understand the classification and the reflection.\n\nActually, I think the key question for the proof is:\n\n**Can we write `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰)·B(u⁰) dμ⁰` where `B(u⁰) = A(u⁰)` (or `B(u⁰) = A(reflect(u⁰))` with a measure-preserving change of variables)?**\n\nIf yes, then `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A² ≥ 0` (or `∫ A(u⁰)·A(reflect(u⁰)) dμ⁰` which needs another argument).\n\nLet me look at the code to understand the reflection of interface links and the structure of the interface action.\n\nActually, I realize I should look at the actual definitions more carefully. Let me search for the key definitions.\n\nLet me look at:\n1. `interfaceSites`, `positiveSites`, `negativeSites`\n2. `reflectLinkVariable` \n3. The interface action decomposition\n\nLet me also look at the `transfer_matrix_fubini_integrated_pull_fullReflect` lemma which is the current state of the art.\n\nActually, I think I should take a step back. I've been doing deep analysis but haven't written any code. Let me think about what the most productive use of this session is.\n\nGiven the deep analysis from session 25, and my analysis above, I think the key insight is:\n\n**If the interface links are fixed by the reflection (u⁰* = u⁰), then the transfer matrix positivity reduces to `∫ A(u⁰)² dμ⁰ ≥ 0`, which is trivially true.**\n\nThe question is whether this is actually the case. Let me check the code.\n\nIf the interface consists only of spatial links at t=0, and these are fixed by the reflection, then u⁰* = u⁰ and the proof is straightforward.\n\nBut if the interface includes temporal links at t=0, and these are mapped to negative links (with inversion) by the reflection, then the situation is more complex.\n\nLet me look at the code to determine this. This is the KEY question that determines the difficulty of the proof.\n\nLet me search for the relevant definitions.","turn":16,"is_prune_marker":false}
