site n and directions μ, ν, the product traverses:\n238 | \n239 |     n + e_μ + e_ν ←── n + e_ν\n240 |          |              ↑\n241 |          |              |\n242 |          ↓              |\n243 |     n + e_μ  ────────── n\n244 | \n\n... 153 more line(s) below (use offset=245 to continue reading)\nTime: 2026-08-05 05:40:17 UTC","timestamp":1785908417,"token_count":692,"tool_call_id":"74b8dcdd-d27e-487b-9d45-52ee8ea92044","full_token_estimate":480,"tool_meta":{"tool_name":"read_file","file_path":"src/lean/YangMills/Lattice.lean","old_text":null,"new_text":null,"exit_code":null,"line_count":397,"byte_count":14494,"is_error":false,"duration_ms":0,"edit_line":null},"turn":21,"is_prune_marker":false}
{"id":44,"role":"Assistant","content":"I'm at 73% context. Let me read the key `reflectToPosInterface` definition and the fullReflect form, then decide on the approach.\nTime: 2026-08-05 05:45:02 UTC","timestamp":1785908702,"token_count":36,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"offset\":1100,\"path\":\"src/lean/YangMills/Proofs/TransferMatrix.lean\",\"limit\":60}","name":"read_file"},"id":"a5829282-20fd-4219-9850-4343a78d9ec1","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":8648,"reasoning_content":"I'm at 73% context. Let me synthesize what I've learned and think about the key insight.\n\nKey findings from reading the code:\n\n1. **Site classification**: \n   - `positiveSites`: signedTime > 0\n   - `negativeSites`: signedTime < 0\n   - `interfaceSites`: signedTime = 0\n\n2. **Reflection** (`reflectLinkVariable`):\n   - `(θU)(n, μ) = U(θn, μ)⁻¹` if μ = 0 (time direction) — INVERSE\n   - `(θU)(n, μ) = U(θn, μ)` if μ ≠ 0 (spatial direction) — NO inverse\n\n3. **Action decomposition**:\n   - `wilsonActionOSPositive`: plaquettes with ALL four corners having signedTime > 0\n   - `wilsonActionOSNegative`: plaquettes with ALL four corners having signedTime < 0\n   - `wilsonActionOSInterface`: all remaining plaquettes (those with corners on both sides)\n\n4. **Reflection is an involution**: θ(θU) = U (proven).\n\nNow, the KEY question: **what does the reflection do to interface links?**\n\nInterface sites have signedTime = 0. The reflection maps signedTime 0 → 0 (interface → interface). So interface sites are fixed by the reflection (as sites).\n\nFor interface links (links starting at interface sites):\n- Spatial interface link U((0,x), i): θ maps to U((0,x), i) (same site, same direction, no inverse). So **spatial interface links are FIXED by the reflection**.\n- Temporal interface link U((0,x), 0): θ maps to U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ (same site, time direction, WITH inverse). So **temporal interface links are INVERTED by the reflection**.\n\nSo the interface links are NOT all fixed by the reflection. The temporal interface links are inverted.\n\nThis means `u⁰* ≠ u⁰` in general — the temporal interface links are inverted.\n\nNow, let me reconsider the key factorization. The interface action S_int consists of plaquettes with corners on both sides of the interface. These include:\n- Time-space plaquettes at t=0: corners at t=0 and t=1 (positive + interface)\n- Time-space plaquettes at t=-1: corners at t=-1 and t=0 (negative + interface)\n- Pure spatial plaquettes at t=0: all corners at t=0 (interface only)\n\nWait, actually, a pure spatial plaquette at t=0 (in the (i,j) plane at site (0,x)) has all four corners at t=0, so signedTime = 0 for all. This is NOT positive (not all > 0) and NOT negative (not all < 0), so it's an INTERFACE plaquette.\n\nSo the interface action includes:\n1. Pure spatial plaquettes at t=0 (all interface links)\n2. Time-space plaquettes at t=0 (positive + interface links)\n3. Time-space plaquettes at t=-1 (negative + interface links)\n\nNow, the reflection maps:\n- Type 1 (spatial at t=0): maps to itself (all corners at t=0, fixed). The plaquette variable is unchanged (spatial links are not inverted).\n- Type 2 (time-space at t=0): maps to type 3 (time-space at t=-1). The temporal links are inverted.\n- Type 3 (time-space at t=-1): maps to type 2.\n\nSo `S_int = S_spatial_0 + S_ts_upper + S_ts_lower` where:\n- `S_spatial_0`: pure spatial plaquettes at t=0 (interface only, reflection-invariant)\n- `S_ts_upper`: time-space plaquettes at t=0 (positive + interface)\n- `S_ts_lower`: time-space plaquettes at t=-1 (negative + interface)\n\nAnd `S_ts_lower(U) = S_ts_upper(θU)` by reflection.\n\nNow, `S_ts_upper` depends on positive links (spatial at t=1) and interface links (temporal at t=0, spatial at t=0).\n`S_ts_lower` depends on negative links (spatial at t=-1, temporal at t=-1) and interface links (spatial at t=0).\n`S_spatial_0` depends only on interface links (spatial at t=0).\n\nSo `S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰) = S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰)`.\n\nNow, going back to the transfer matrix:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nWait, I had this before. Let me redo with the decomposition:\n`S_int(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰) = S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰)`\n\nSo:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2 + S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`\n\nThe inner integral over U⁻ does NOT depend on u⁺! So:\n`(Tg)(u⁺, u⁰) = exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2 + S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\nwhere `B(u⁰) = ∫ f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰))) dμ⁻`.\n\nAnd:\n`g(u⁺, u⁰) · T(g)(u⁺, u⁰) = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰)/2 + S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\n`= f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u⁺, u⁰) + S_spatial_0(u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\nNow, `S⁺(u⁺, u⁰) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰)` is the action of all plaquettes involving positive links (both purely positive and upper interface). Let me call this `S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)`.\n\nSo:\n`g·Tg = f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰) + S_spatial_0(u⁰))) · B(u⁰)`\n\nAnd:\n`∫ g·Tg dμ⁺⁰ = ∫ [∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺] · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰)) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nLet `A(u⁰) = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`.\n\nThen:\n`∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰)) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nNow, by the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺) (measure-preserving):\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) + S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰))) dμ⁺`\n\nBy reflection symmetry:\n- `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S⁺(V⁺, θ(u⁰))` where θ(u⁰) is the reflected interface links.\n\nWait, I need to be careful. `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰)` is the negative action at the config with negative links = reflect(V⁺) and interface links = u⁰. By the reflection symmetry `S⁻(θU) = S⁺(U)`:\n`S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S⁺(θ(reflect(V⁺), u⁰))`\n\nNow, `θ(reflect(V⁺), u⁰)` is the full reflection of the config with positive links = reflect(V⁺), negative links = ?, interface links = u⁰. But we don't have negative links here...\n\nActually, I think the issue is that `S⁻` only depends on negative and interface links. So `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰)` is the negative action at a config where the negative links are reflect(V⁺) and the interface links are u⁰. The positive links don't matter for S⁻.\n\nBy the reflection symmetry: the negative action at (U⁻, u⁰) equals the positive action at (reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)):\n`S⁻(U⁻, u⁰) = S⁺(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰))`\n\nwhere θ(u⁰) is the reflected interface links (temporal links inverted, spatial links fixed).\n\nSo `S⁻(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S⁺(V⁺, θ(u⁰))`.\n\nSimilarly, `S_ts_lower(reflect(V⁺), u⁰) = S_ts_upper(V⁺, θ(u⁰))` (lower interface at reflected = upper interface at original with reflected interface).\n\nSo:\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·(S⁺(V⁺, θ(u⁰)) + S_ts_upper(V⁺, θ(u⁰)))) dμ⁺`\n`= ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, θ(u⁰))) dμ⁺`\n\nNow, this is NOT `A(u⁰)` because:\n- f has `u⁰` (original interface links)\n- The exponential has `θ(u⁰)` (reflected interface links)\n\nAnd it's NOT `A(θ(u⁰))` because:\n- f has `u⁰` (not θ(u⁰))\n- A(θ(u⁰)) would have f(V⁺, θ(u⁰))\n\nSo `B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, θ(u⁰))) dμ⁰`.\n\nThis is a \"mixed\" expression where f is at u⁰ but the exponential is at θ(u⁰). This is the fundamental obstruction!\n\nIf θ(u⁰) = u⁰ (interface links fixed by reflection), then B(u⁰) = A(u⁰) and we're done. But θ(u⁰) ≠ u⁰ because the temporal interface links are inverted.\n\nSo the obstruction is the TEMPORAL INTERFACE LINKS. The reflection inverts them, creating a mismatch between f (at u⁰) and the exponential (at θ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, let me think about how to handle this. The temporal interface links are the temporal links at t=0, which go from t=0 to t=1. Under reflection, they map to the temporal links at t=-1 (inverted), which are negative links.\n\nWait, actually, I need to reconsider. The interface links are links STARTING at interface sites (signedTime = 0). A temporal link at (0, x) starts at (0, x) and goes to (1, x). Under reflection, (0, x) → (0, x) and (1, x) → (-1, x). So the link from (0,x) to (1,x) maps to the link from (0,x) to (-1,x), which is the INVERSE of the link from (-1,x) to (0,x).\n\nBut the link from (-1,x) to (0,x) starts at (-1,x), which has signedTime < 0, so it's a NEGATIVE link. So the temporal interface link maps to the inverse of a negative link.\n\nSo `θ(U((0,x), 0)) = U((-1,x), 0)⁻¹`.\n\nNow, `U((-1,x), 0)` is a negative link (starts at t=-1). So the reflection maps the temporal interface link to the inverse of a negative link.\n\nThis means: when we reflect the interface config u⁰, the temporal interface links become negative links (inverted). So `θ(u⁰)` is NOT an interface config — it's a mix of interface (spatial) and negative (temporal) links.\n\nHmm, this complicates the analysis. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that the \"interface\" in the code is defined by the STARTING site of the link. A link at site n in direction μ is an \"interface link\" if n is an interface site (signedTime = 0). This includes:\n- Spatial links at t=0: U((0,x), i) for i = 1, 2, 3\n- Temporal links at t=0: U((0,x), 0)\n\nThe reflection maps:\n- Spatial link at (0,x), direction i → U((0,x), i) (fixed, because (0,x) → (0,x) and i ≠ 0)\n- Temporal link at (0,x), direction 0 → U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ (because (0,x) → (0,x) and μ = 0, so inverse)\n\nWait! The reflection formula is `(θU)(n, μ) = U(θn, μ)⁻¹` if μ = 0, else `U(θn, μ)`. For an interface site n = (0, x), θn = (0, x) (since signedTime 0 → 0). So:\n- `(θU)((0,x), 0) = U((0,x), 0)⁻¹` — temporal interface link is INVERTED\n- `(θU)((0,x), i) = U((0,x), i)` — spatial interface link is FIXED\n\nSo the reflection maps:\n- Temporal interface links → INVERTED (but still at interface sites)\n- Spatial interface links → FIXED\n\nSo `θ(u⁰)` IS an interface config — it's u⁰ with the temporal links inverted and the spatial links fixed. So `θ(u⁰)` is still in the interface space!\n\nThis is important. The reflection maps interface links to interface links (with inversion for temporal). So `θ(u⁰)` is a valid interface config.\n\nNow, the reflection is an involution on the interface config space: `θ(θ(u⁰)) = u⁰` (because inverting twice gives back the original).\n\nAnd the reflection is measure-preserving on the interface config space (because inversion on SU(N) is measure-preserving, and the spatial links are fixed).\n\nSo the change of variables `u⁰ → θ(u⁰)` is measure-preserving.\n\nNow, going back:\n`∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰)) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n\nwhere:\n`A(u⁰) = ∫ f(u⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`\n`B(u⁰) = ∫ f(V⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, θ(u⁰))) dμ⁺`\n\nNow, let me substitute `u⁰ → θ(u⁰)` in the integral (measure-preserving):\n`∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(θ(u⁰))) · B(θ(u⁰)) dμ⁰`\n\nNow, `S_spatial_0(θ(u⁰))` — the pure spatial plaquettes at t=0, evaluated at the reflected interface config. Since the spatial links are fixed by reflection, `S_spatial_0(θ(u⁰)) = S_spatial_0(u⁰)`.\n\nAnd `B(θ(u⁰)) = ∫ f(V⁺, θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, θ(θ(u⁰)))) dμ⁺ = ∫ f(V⁺, θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`.\n\nAnd `A(θ(u⁰)) = ∫ f(u⁺, θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, θ(u⁰))) dμ⁺`.\n\nHmm, so `B(θ(u⁰)) = ∫ f(V⁺, θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(V⁺, u⁰)) dμ⁺`.\n\nThis is NOT `A(u⁰)` because f has `θ(u⁰)` and the exponential has `u⁰`.\n\nAnd `A(θ(u⁰)) = ∫ f(u⁺, θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_pos_total(u⁺, θ(u⁰))) dμ⁺`.\n\nSo `B(θ(u⁰)) ≠ A(θ(u⁰))` and `B(θ(u⁰)) ≠ A(u⁰)`.\n\nThe mismatch is: in B, f is at θ(u⁰) but the exponential is at u⁰. In A, both f and the exponential are at the same interface config.\n\nSo the fundamental issue is that B has f and the exponential at DIFFERENT interface configs, while A has them at the SAME interface config.\n\nThis is the crux of the obstruction. The temporal interface links being inverted by the reflection creates this mismatch.\n\nNow, let me think about how to resolve this. \n\n**Key idea**: The temporal interface links appear in the upper interface plaquettes (S_ts_upper) and the temporal plaquette factors. The PD property of the plaquette factor might help here.\n\nLet me think about what S_pos_total looks like. It's the action of all plaquettes involving positive links:\n- Pure positive plaquettes (all corners at t > 0): these involve only positive links.\n- Upper interface plaquettes (time-space at t=0): these involve positive links (spatial at t=1) and interface links (temporal at t=0, spatial at t=0).\n\nThe upper interface plaquettes couple the positive links and the interface links. The key: the temporal interface links appear in these plaquettes.\n\nNow, the temporal interface link U((0,x), 0) appears in the upper interface plaquette at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane:\nU_p = U((0,x), 0) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†\n\nWait, U((1,x+î), 0) is a temporal link at t=1, which is a POSITIVE link (starts at t=1 > 0). And U((1,x), i) is a spatial link at t=1, also positive. And U((0,x), 0) is a temporal interface link, and U((0,x), i) is a spatial interface link.\n\nSo the upper interface plaquette involves: 1 temporal interface link, 2 positive links, 1 spatial interface link.\n\nNow, the key: the temporal interface link U((0,x), 0) appears LINEARLY in the plaquette (as one of the 4 factors). The plaquette Boltzmann factor `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` is a PD function of the plaquette variable U_p.\n\nBut U_p is a PRODUCT of 4 links, so the Boltzmann factor is a function of the product, not of the individual links. The PD property is about the plaquette variable, not the individual links.\n\nHmm, so the PD property of the plaquette factor doesn't directly help with the temporal interface link mismatch.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The key insight from the Lüscher approach is:\n\n**The transfer matrix can be decomposed as T = V^{1/2} · U · V^{1/2} where V is positive (spatial) and U is positive (temporal PD kernel).**\n\nIn our case, the \"spatial\" part V includes the spatial plaquette factors (which are positive functions), and the \"temporal\" part U includes the temporal plaquette factors (which are PD kernels).\n\nThe key: the temporal plaquette factor is a PD kernel in the SPATIAL link variables (at consecutive times), with the temporal links as parameters. The temporal interface links are PARAMETERS of this kernel, not variables.\n\nSo the PD property of the temporal plaquette factor is in the spatial links, NOT in the temporal links. The temporal links (including the temporal interface links) are fixed parameters.\n\nThis means: the mismatch in the temporal interface links (u⁰ vs θ(u⁰)) doesn't affect the PD property, because the PD property is in the spatial links, and the temporal links are just parameters.\n\nLet me make this more precise. The temporal plaquette factor at the interface is:\n`K(U⁺_spatial, U⁻_spatial, u⁰; W) = ∏_x exp(β·Re Tr(U_p(x)))`\n\nwhere U_p(x) is the temporal plaquette at x, involving spatial links at t=0 and t=±1, and temporal links.\n\nFor fixed temporal links (including temporal interface links u⁰_temporal), K is a PD kernel in the spatial link variables (U⁺_spatial, U⁻_spatial, u⁰_spatial).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral (after absorbing the spatial plaquette factors) is:\n`I = ∫ g(U⁺, u⁰) · g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ`\n\nWait, but g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) — the function g evaluated at the reflected config. The reflected config has positive links = reflect(U⁻) and interface links = θ(u⁰). So g is evaluated at (reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)).\n\nNow, g(U⁺, u⁰) = f(U⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺, u⁰)/2) where S_spatial⁺ is the spatial plaquette factor in the positive half.\n\nAnd g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰))/2) = f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2) (by reflection symmetry of the spatial action).\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰) · f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S_spatial⁺(U⁺, u⁰) + S_spatial⁻(U⁻, u⁰))/2) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ`\n\nNow, K_temporal is a PD kernel in the spatial link variables. The integral is:\n`I = ∫∫ f(U⁺, u⁰) · f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · [spatial factor] · K_temporal dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰`\n\nThe spatial factor `exp(-β·(S_spatial⁺ + S_spatial⁻)/2)` can be absorbed into f:\n`f̃(U⁺, u⁰) = f(U⁺, u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁺(U⁺, u⁰)/2)`\n`f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S_spatial⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2)`\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰`\n\nNow, K_temporal is a PD kernel in (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_spatial) for fixed (u⁰_temporal, W). The integral is:\n`I = ∫_{u⁰} ∫∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰`\n\nFor fixed u⁰, the inner double integral is:\n`J(u⁰) = ∫∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰; W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻`\n\nNow, K_temporal is a PD kernel in (U⁺, U⁻) for fixed (u⁰, W). The PD property means:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · conj(h(U⁻)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻; u⁰, W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nBut our integral has `f̃(U⁺, u⁰) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰))`, which is NOT of the form `h(U⁺) · conj(h(U⁻))` because:\n1. f̃(U⁺, u⁰) depends on u⁰ (not just U⁺)\n2. f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) depends on θ(u⁰) (not just U⁻)\n3. The two factors are at DIFFERENT interface configs (u⁰ vs θ(u⁰))\n\nSo even with the Lüscher decomposition, we still face the mismatch.\n\nHmm. But wait — the Lüscher approach doesn't integrate over the interface links in this way. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think the issue is that I'm conflating two different formulations. Let me think about the Lüscher transfer matrix more carefully.\n\nIn the Lüscher formulation, the transfer matrix acts on L²(G^S) where S is the set of SPATIAL links at a SINGLE time slice. The transfer matrix for a single time step is:\n`(T_step φ)(U) = ∫ K(U, V) φ(V) dV`\n\nwhere K(U, V) is the kernel for a single time step, and U, V are spatial link configs at consecutive times.\n\nThe key: the temporal links are INTEGRATED OUT (or fixed as parameters) in the transfer matrix. The transfer matrix only acts on spatial links.\n\nIn our formulation, the \"transfer matrix\" `transferMatrixCorrect` acts on functions of ALL positive + interface links, and it integrates out the negative links. This is a DIFFERENT object.\n\nThe Lüscher transfer matrix is a single-step operator, while ours is a \"half-space\" operator. They're related by:\n`T_half = T_step^{n}` (n-th power of the single-step transfer matrix, where n is the number of time slices in the positive half).\n\nAnd the reflection positivity is `⟨f, T_half f⟩ ≥ 0`, which follows from T_step being positive (since T_half = T_step^n is positive if T_step is positive).\n\nBut to formalize this, we'd need to:\n1. Define the single-step transfer matrix T_step.\n2. Show T_step is positive (using the Lüscher decomposition V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}).\n3. Show T_half = T_step^n.\n4. Conclude T_half is positive.\n\nThis is a significant restructuring. But it might be cleaner than the L² expansion + reorganization.\n\nHowever, there's a subtlety: the interface. The Lüscher transfer matrix acts on spatial links at a single time slice. But our \"interface\" includes both spatial and temporal links at t=0. The temporal interface links are not part of the Lüscher Hilbert space.\n\nHmm, let me think about this. In the Lüscher formulation, the Hilbert space at each time slice is L²(G^S) where S is the spatial links. The temporal links are part of the transfer matrix kernel (they're the \"hopping\" parameters).\n\nSo the temporal interface links would be part of the transfer matrix kernel, not the Hilbert space. The spatial interface links would be part of the Hilbert space at t=0.\n\nThis means the Lüscher transfer matrix acts on functions of the SPATIAL interface links (and the positive spatial links), not the temporal interface links.\n\nBut our `transferMatrixCorrect` acts on functions of ALL positive + interface links (including temporal interface links). So there's a mismatch.\n\nTo use the Lüscher approach, we'd need to:\n1. Integrate out the temporal interface links first (treating them as part of the transfer matrix kernel).\n2. Then apply the Lüscher decomposition to the remaining spatial links.\n\nBut integrating out the temporal interface links is non-trivial because they appear in the interface plaquettes.\n\nOK, I think I've done enough analysis. Let me summarize the key findings and decide on the next steps.\n\n**Summary of analysis:**\n\n1. The reflection maps:\n   - Spatial interface links → FIXED (θ(u⁰_spatial) = u⁰_spatial)\n   - Temporal interface links → INVERTED (θ(u⁰_temporal) = u⁰_temporal⁻¹)\n\n2. The interface action decomposes as:\n   - S_spatial_0(u⁰): pure spatial plaquettes at t=0 (interface only, reflection-invariant)\n   - S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰): time-space plaquettes at t=0 (positive + interface)\n   - S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰): time-space plaquettes at t=-1 (negative + interface)\n\n3. The transfer matrix positivity reduces to:\n   `∫ g·Tg = ∫ A(u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰)) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰`\n   where A and B involve f and the positive action at DIFFERENT interface configs (u⁰ vs θ(u⁰)).\n\n4. The mismatch `u⁰ vs θ(u⁰)` is due to the temporal interface links being inverted by the reflection.\n\n5. The Lüscher approach (T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) would work if we could separate the spatial and temporal interface links, treating the temporal interface links as parameters of the PD kernel.\n\n**The key insight for a potential proof:**\n\nThe temporal interface links appear in the temporal plaquette factors, which are PD kernels in the SPATIAL link variables. The PD property is in the spatial links, with the temporal links (including temporal interface links) as parameters.\n\nSo the approach would be:\n1. Fix the temporal interface links u⁰_temporal (as parameters).\n2. The temporal plaquette factor is a PD kernel in the spatial link variables (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_spatial).\n3. The reflection positivity integral (for fixed u⁰_temporal) is a PD quadratic form in the spatial links.\n4. Integrate over u⁰_temporal (the result is non-negative for each fixed u⁰_temporal, so the integral is non-negative).\n\nBut step 3 requires that the two f-factors are at the SAME spatial interface config. Since the spatial interface links are FIXED by the reflection (θ(u⁰_spatial) = u⁰_spatial), this IS the case!\n\nSo the key insight is: **the spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection, so the PD quadratic form in the spatial links works. The temporal interface links are parameters, and the result is non-negative for each fixed value of the temporal interface links.**\n\nLet me make this more precise. For fixed temporal interface links w = u⁰_temporal:\n\n`I(w) = ∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰_spatial, w) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_spatial, w⁻¹) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_spatial; w, W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_spatial`\n\nWait, θ(u⁰) = (u⁰_spatial, w⁻¹) because spatial links are fixed and temporal links are inverted. So f̃(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_spatial, w⁻¹).\n\nHmm, so the second f̃ has temporal interface links w⁻¹, not w. So the two f̃'s are at DIFFERENT temporal interface configs (w vs w⁻¹).\n\nThis is still a mismatch! The temporal interface links are inverted, so the two f̃'s are at w and w⁻¹.\n\nBut wait — the temporal plaquette factor K_temporal also depends on w. Let me think about how w appears in K_temporal.\n\nThe temporal plaquette at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane is:\nU_p = U((0,x), 0) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†\n\nHere, U((0,x), 0) is the temporal interface link w(x), and U((1,x+î), 0) is a positive temporal link. The plaquette Boltzmann factor is `exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†))`.\n\nNow, the reflected plaquette at (-1, x) in the (0, i) plane is:\nU_p' = U((-1,x), 0) · U((0,x), i) · U((0,x+î), 0)† · U((-1,x), i)†\n\nHere, U((0,x+î), 0)† = w(x+î)⁻¹ (inverted temporal interface link). And U((-1,x), 0) is a negative temporal link, U((-1,x), i) is a negative spatial link.\n\nBy the reflection symmetry, the plaquette at (-1, x) is the mirror of the plaquette at (0, x), and `S_p(θU) = S_p(U)` (the action is reflection-invariant). So:\n`Re Tr(U_p') = Re Tr(U_p)` when U' = θU.\n\nThis means the temporal plaquette factor at (-1, x) involves w(x+î)⁻¹, while the one at (0, x) involves w(x). They're related by the reflection.\n\nNow, the full temporal plaquette factor K_temporal is a product over all time-space plaquettes. For the interface, it includes both the upper (t=0) and lower (t=-1) plaquettes. The upper plaquettes involve w(x), and the lower plaquettes involve w(x)⁻¹ (through the reflection).\n\nSo K_temporal depends on BOTH w and w⁻¹. The PD property of K_temporal is in the spatial link variables, with (w, w⁻¹) as parameters.\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral for fixed w is:\n`I(w) = ∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s; w, w⁻¹, W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nThe two f̃'s are at (u⁰_s, w) and (u⁰_s, w⁻¹) — same spatial interface links, different temporal interface links.\n\nThe PD property of K_temporal is in (U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) for fixed (w, w⁻¹, W). The PD quadratic form would be:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(h(U⁻, u⁰_s)) · K_temporal(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s; w, w⁻¹, W) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s ≥ 0`\n\nBut our integral has `f̃(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹)`, which is NOT `h(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(h(U⁻, u⁰_s))` because:\n1. The first f̃ depends on w (not just spatial links)\n2. The second f̃ depends on w⁻¹ (not just spatial links)\n3. The two f̃'s are at different temporal interface configs\n\nSo even for fixed w, the integral is NOT a standard PD quadratic form.\n\nHmm. The issue is that f̃ depends on the temporal interface links w, and the two f̃'s are at w and w⁻¹.\n\nBut wait — if f depends only on POSITIVE and INTERFACE links, and the temporal interface links are part of the interface, then f DOES depend on w. And the two f̃'s are at w and w⁻¹.\n\nSo the mismatch is fundamental: the reflection inverts the temporal interface links, creating a mismatch between the two f̃'s.\n\nNow, let me think about how the standard proof handles this. In the Osterwalder-Seiler proof, the key is that the temporal interface links are integrated out using the PD property of the plaquette factor, not treated as parameters.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is different. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U) · f(θU) · exp(-β·S_W(U)) dμ`\n\nwhere f depends on positive + interface links, and θU is the reflected config.\n\nNow, f(θU) = f(θ(U⁺), θ(u⁰)) where θ(U⁺) are the reflected positive links (which are negative links) and θ(u⁰) = (u⁰_s, w⁻¹) (spatial fixed, temporal inverted).\n\nBut f depends on positive + interface links. θ(U⁺) are negative links, so f(θU) = f(θ(U⁺), θ(u⁰)) — but f is defined on positive + interface links, and θ(U⁺) are negative links. How does f evaluate at negative links?\n\nAh, I think the key is that f is extended to all links. The function f is defined on all link variables, but it's SUPPORTED on positive + interface links (i.e., it only depends on positive + interface links). So f(θU) = f(θ(U⁺), θ(u⁰), U⁻) — but since f only depends on positive + interface links, f(θU) = f(positive links of θU, interface links of θU).\n\nThe positive links of θU are θ(U⁻) (reflected negative links), and the interface links of θU are θ(u⁰) = (u⁰_s, w⁻¹).\n\nSo f(θU) = f(θ(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹).\n\nAnd f(U) = f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w).\n\nSo the two f's are:\n- f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) — at positive links U⁺, spatial interface u⁰_s, temporal interface w\n- f(θ(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) — at reflected negative links θ(U⁻), spatial interface u⁰_s, temporal interface w⁻¹\n\nThe spatial interface links are the SAME (u⁰_s), but the temporal interface links are DIFFERENT (w vs w⁻¹).\n\nNow, the Boltzmann factor exp(-β·S_W) is a product of plaquette factors. The temporal interface links w appear in the interface plaquettes. The key: the interface plaquettes involve w (in the upper plaquettes) and w⁻¹ (in the lower plaquettes, through the reflection).\n\nActually, let me think about this more carefully. The temporal interface link w(x) = U((0,x), 0) appears in:\n1. Upper interface plaquettes at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane: U_p = w(x) · U((1,x), i) · ... · U((0,x), i)†\n2. Upper interface plaquettes at (0, x-î) in the (0, i) plane: U_p = w(x-î) · ... · w(x)† · ... \n\nHmm, actually, the temporal interface link w(x) = U((0,x), 0) appears in plaquettes where it's one of the 4 links. These are the time-space plaquettes at (0, x) (where w(x) is the \"first\" link) and at (0, x-î) (where w(x) is the \"third\" link, inverted).\n\nAnd the reflected link w(x)⁻¹ = U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ appears in the lower interface plaquettes at (-1, x) (where it's the \"third\" link, which is w(x)⁻¹ because the reflection inverts it).\n\nWait, I'm getting confused. Let me think about it differently.\n\nThe full Boltzmann factor is a product over ALL plaquettes. The temporal interface links w appear in the interface plaquettes. The key: the interface plaquettes come in pairs (upper and lower), related by reflection. The upper plaquette involves w, and the lower plaquette involves w⁻¹ (through the reflection).\n\nSo the Boltzmann factor, restricted to the interface plaquettes, is:\n`exp(-β·S_int) = exp(-β·(S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰_s, w)))`\n\nwhere S_ts_lower involves w through the lower plaquettes. But actually, the lower plaquettes involve the NEGATIVE temporal links and the spatial interface links, not the temporal interface links directly.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The lower interface plaquette at (-1, x) in the (0, i) plane is:\nU_p = U((-1,x), 0) · U((0,x), i) · U((-1,x+î), 0)† · U((-1,x), i)†\n\nThis involves:\n- U((-1,x), 0): negative temporal link\n- U((0,x), i): spatial interface link\n- U((-1,x+î), 0)†: negative temporal link (inverted)\n- U((-1,x), i)†: negative spatial link (inverted)\n\nSo the lower interface plaquette does NOT involve the temporal interface link w(x) = U((0,x), 0). It involves negative temporal links and spatial interface links.\n\nAnd the upper interface plaquette at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane is:\nU_p = U((0,x), 0) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†\n\nThis involves:\n- U((0,x), 0) = w(x): temporal interface link\n- U((1,x), i): positive spatial link\n- U((1,x+î), 0)†: positive temporal link (inverted)\n- U((0,x), i)†: spatial interface link (inverted)\n\nSo the upper interface plaquette involves the temporal interface link w(x), positive links, and spatial interface links.\n\nSo the temporal interface links w appear ONLY in the upper interface plaquettes (and the purely positive temporal plaquettes at t > 0, but those are part of S⁺).\n\nNow, the reflection maps the upper interface plaquette at (0, x) to the lower interface plaquette at (-1, x). The upper plaquette involves w(x), and the lower plaquette involves U((-1,x), 0) (negative temporal link). By the reflection, w(x) = U((0,x), 0) maps to U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ = U((-1,x), 0) (wait, that's not right).\n\nActually, the reflection maps U((0,x), 0) to U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ (as we established). But U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ is the inverse of the temporal interface link, which is NOT a negative link — it's still an interface link (at the same site (0,x)).\n\nHmm, but the lower interface plaquette at (-1, x) involves U((-1,x), 0), which is a negative temporal link, NOT U((0,x), 0)⁻¹.\n\nSo the reflection does NOT map the upper plaquette's temporal interface link to the lower plaquette's temporal link. Instead, it maps U((0,x), 0) to U((0,x), 0)⁻¹, which is still at the interface.\n\nI think I'm confusing myself. Let me re-examine the reflection of the plaquette.\n\nThe reflection θ maps the plaquette at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane to the plaquette at (0, x) in the (0, i) plane... no, it maps to the plaquette at θ(0, x) = (0, x) in the (0, i) plane. But the links are reflected.\n\nActually, the reflection maps the plaquette U_p(0, x, 0, i) = U((0,x), 0) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)† to:\nθ(U_p) = (θU)((0,x), 0) · (θU)((1,x), i) · (θU)((1,x+î), 0)† · (θU)((0,x), i)†\n\nWait, but the reflection maps the SITE (0, x) to (0, x) and (1, x) to (-1, x). So the plaquette at (0, x) maps to the plaquette at (0, x), but with the links reflected:\n- (θU)((0,x), 0) = U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ = w(x)⁻¹\n- (θU)((1,x), i) = U((-1,x), i) (spatial, no inverse)\n- (θU)((1,x+î), 0)† = U((-1,x+î), 0) (temporal, inverse of inverse = no inverse... wait)\n\nHmm, let me be more careful. (θU)(n, μ) = U(θn, μ)⁻¹ if μ = 0, else U(θn, μ).\n\nSo:\n- (θU)((0,x), 0) = U(θ(0,x), 0)⁻¹ = U((0,x), 0)⁻¹ = w(x)⁻¹\n- (θU)((1,x), i) = U(θ(1,x), i) = U((-1,x), i) (i ≠ 0, no inverse)\n- (θU)((1,x+î), 0) = U(θ(1,x+î), 0)⁻¹ = U((-1,x+î), 0)⁻¹\n- (θU)((0,x), i) = U(θ(0,x), i) = U((0,x), i) (i ≠ 0, no inverse)\n\nSo the reflected plaquette is:\nθ(U_p) = w(x)⁻¹ · U((-1,x), i) · (U((-1,x+î), 0)⁻¹)† · U((0,x), i)†\n= w(x)⁻¹ · U((-1,x), i) · U((-1,x+î), 0) · U((0,x), i)†\n\nAnd the plaquette at (-1, x) in the (0, i) plane is:\nU_p(-1, x, 0, i) = U((-1,x), 0) · U((0,x), i) · U((-1,x+î), 0)† · U((-1,x), i)†\n\nThese are NOT the same. The reflected plaquette θ(U_p(0,x)) involves w(x)⁻¹, while the plaquette at (-1, x) involves U((-1,x), 0).\n\nSo the reflection does NOT map the upper interface plaquette to the lower interface plaquette. Instead, it maps the upper interface plaquette to a plaquette at the SAME site (0, x) but with reflected links.\n\nThis means the reflection maps the upper interface plaquette to ANOTHER upper interface plaquette (at the same site, but with reflected links). And the action satisfies S_p(θU) = S_p(U) (reflection invariance of the action), which means:\n`Re Tr(θ(U_p)) = Re Tr(U_p)`\n`Re Tr(w(x)⁻¹ · U((-1,x), i) · U((-1,x+î), 0) · U((0,x), i)†) = Re Tr(w(x) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†)`\n\nThis is the reflection invariance of the plaquette action.\n\nOK, I think I've been going down a rabbit hole. Let me step back and think about the high-level strategy.\n\nI'm at 73% context. I've done extensive analysis but haven't written any code. Let me think about what the most productive next step is.\n\nThe key findings are:\n1. The spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection.\n2. The temporal interface links are inverted by the reflection.\n3. The mismatch between u⁰ and θ(u⁰) is due to the temporal interface links.\n4. The Lüscher approach (T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) requires separating spatial and temporal interface links.\n\nThe most promising approach seems to be:\n- **Integrate out the temporal interface links first**, using the PD property of the plaquette factor.\n- Then the remaining integral over spatial links is a standard PD quadratic form.\n\nBut this requires understanding how the temporal interface links appear in the Boltzmann factor and how to integrate them out.\n\nActually, let me think about this from a different angle. The key identity is:\n`∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g·Tg dμ⁺⁰`\n\nAnd we need to show `∫ g·Tg ≥ 0`.\n\nThe transfer matrix T is:\n`(Tg)(u) = ∫ g(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁺(u)/2 + S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻`\n\nThe key: `g(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰))` — g evaluated at the reflected config. The reflected config has positive links = reflect(U⁻) and interface links = u⁰ (the SAME interface links as u).\n\nWait, is that right? Let me check `reflectToPosInterface`.\n\nLet me read the definition of `reflectToPosInterface`.","turn":23,"is_prune_marker":false}
