{"id":46,"role":"Assistant","content":"I'm at 81% context. I've done deep analysis this session. Let me document the key findings and prepare for handoff. First, let me verify the build is still green.\nTime: 2026-08-05 05:48:52 UTC","timestamp":1785908932,"token_count":49,"tool_calls":[{"function":{"arguments":"{\"command\":\"powershell -Command \\\"lake build YangMills.Proofs.TransferMatrix 2>&1 | Select-String -Pattern 'error:|Build completed' | Select-Object -Last 5\\\"\",\"timeout_secs\":300}","name":"run_shell"},"id":"578cbad3-c6d3-441c-a52b-4467409b9f15","type":"function"}],"full_token_estimate":10587,"reasoning_content":"I'm at 81% context. Let me understand `reflectToPosInterface`:\n\nIt takes U⁻ (negative links) and U⁰ (interface links), creates a full config with positive links = 1 (identity), negative links = U⁻, interface links = U⁰, then reflects the full config, and restricts to positive+interface.\n\nThe reflection maps:\n- Positive links (all 1) → negative links (all 1, since θ(1) = 1)\n- Negative links (U⁻) → positive links (reflect(U⁻))\n- Interface links (U⁰) → interface links (θ(U⁰), with temporal inverted)\n\nSo `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, U⁰)` = (reflect(U⁻) on positive links, θ(U⁰) on interface links).\n\nThe key: the interface links in the result are θ(U⁰), NOT U⁰. So the temporal interface links are INVERTED.\n\nThis confirms my analysis: `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)` has interface links = θ(u⁰) = (u⁰_spatial, u⁰_temporal⁻¹).\n\nSo in the transfer matrix:\n`(Tg)(u) = ∫ g(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(...) dμ⁻`\n`= ∫ g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(...) dμ⁻`\n\nAnd `g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁺(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰))/2)`.\n\nBy reflection symmetry: `S⁺(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)` (positive action at reflected config = negative action at original).\n\nSo `g(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) = f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) · exp(-β·S⁻(U⁻, u⁰)/2)`.\n\nNow, the key: f is evaluated at (reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)), which has temporal interface links INVERTED compared to u.\n\nSo the two f's in the reflection positivity integral are:\n- f(U⁺, u⁰) — at (U⁺, u⁰)\n- f(reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰)) — at (reflect(U⁻), θ(u⁰))\n\nThe spatial interface links are the SAME (u⁰_spatial), but the temporal interface links are DIFFERENT (u⁰_temporal vs u⁰_temporal⁻¹).\n\nThis is the fundamental mismatch. The temporal interface links are inverted by the reflection.\n\nNow, I've been thinking about this for a while. Let me consider the possibility that the proof requires a DIFFERENT decomposition of the action — one that treats the temporal interface links differently.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the Lüscher approach. In the Lüscher approach, the transfer matrix acts on functions of SPATIAL links only (at a single time slice). The temporal links are part of the kernel.\n\nIf we could reformulate the transfer matrix to act on functions of SPATIAL interface links only (integrating out the temporal interface links), then the reflection would fix the spatial interface links, and the PD property would apply.\n\nBut this requires integrating out the temporal interface links, which is non-trivial.\n\nAlternatively, maybe the proof uses a different reflection plane. If the reflection plane is between t=0 and t=1 (a \"link plane\" rather than a \"site plane\"), then the interface would consist of temporal links only (the links crossing the plane), and the spatial links would be either positive or negative.\n\nActually, the paper I found (\"General proof of Osterwalder-Schrader positivity for the Wilson action\") extends the proof to \"planes containing sites\" (which is our case). The original Osterwalder-Seiler proof was for \"planes between sites\" (link planes). The link plane case might be easier because the interface consists of temporal links only, and the spatial links are cleanly separated.\n\nBut our code uses a site plane (signedTime = 0 is the interface). So we're in the harder case.\n\nOK, I think I've done enough analysis for this session. I'm at 81% context. Let me summarize my findings, update the design doc, and prepare for handoff.\n\nThe key insight from this session is:\n\n**The reflection positivity proof reduces to showing `∫ A(u⁰) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰)) · B(u⁰) dμ⁰ ≥ 0` where A and B involve f at different temporal interface configs (u⁰ vs θ(u⁰)). The mismatch is due to the temporal interface links being inverted by the reflection. The spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection.**\n\n**The most promising approach is to integrate out the temporal interface links using the PD property of the temporal plaquette factor, treating them as part of the PD kernel (not as variables of the function f). This would leave a PD quadratic form in the spatial links, which is non-negative.**\n\n**Alternatively, the Lüscher transfer matrix approach (T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) would work if we reformulate the transfer matrix to act on spatial links only, with temporal links as kernel parameters.**\n\nLet me document this in the design doc and prepare for handoff. Let me also check the build is still green.\n\nActually, wait. Let me think about one more thing. The key question is: can we integrate out the temporal interface links?\n\nThe temporal interface links w = u⁰_temporal appear in:\n1. The function f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) — f depends on w.\n2. The function f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) — f depends on w⁻¹.\n3. The upper interface plaquettes S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) — depends on w.\n4. The purely positive temporal plaquettes (part of S⁺) — these involve positive temporal links, not w.\n\nWait, actually, the temporal interface links w also appear in the temporal plaquettes at t=0 (the upper interface plaquettes). And the reflected w⁻¹ appears in... hmm, the lower interface plaquettes don't involve w directly (they involve negative temporal links).\n\nSo w appears in:\n- f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)\n- f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹)\n- S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w) (upper interface plaquettes)\n- S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰_s) — does NOT depend on w (lower plaquettes involve negative temporal links, not w)\n\nWait, but the lower interface plaquettes at (-1, x) involve U((-1,x), 0) (negative temporal link) and U((0,x), i) (spatial interface link). They do NOT involve w(x) = U((0,x), 0).\n\nSo w appears only in the upper interface plaquettes and in f.\n\nNow, the upper interface plaquettes involve w, positive spatial links (at t=1), positive temporal links (at t=1), and spatial interface links. The Boltzmann factor for the upper interface plaquettes is:\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(u⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∏_x exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)†))`\n\nThis is a product over x of plaquette factors, each involving w(x) and positive/interface links.\n\nNow, the key: for fixed positive links and spatial interface links, the upper interface plaquette factor is a function of w. And the PD property of the plaquette factor means... hmm, the plaquette factor `exp(β·Re Tr(w · A))` (where A is the product of the other 3 links) is a PD function of w? \n\nActually, `exp(β·Re Tr(w · A))` is a PD function of w for fixed A, because `Re Tr(w · A) = Re Tr(A · w)` and `exp(β·Re Tr(B · w))` is PD in w (it's the plaquette Boltzmann factor with 3 links fixed).\n\nWait, is `exp(β·Re Tr(B · w))` PD in w? The PD property of the plaquette factor says `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` is PD in U_p = w · A. But U_p = w · A is a product, and PD in U_p doesn't directly imply PD in w.\n\nHowever, `exp(β·Re Tr(w · A))` = `exp(β·Re Tr(A · w))` and this is a class function of w only if A is the identity. In general, it's NOT a class function of w.\n\nBut the PD property of `exp(β·Re Tr(B · w))` in w (for fixed B) can be seen from the Peter-Weyl expansion:\n`exp(β·Re Tr(B · w)) = ∑_λ c_λ(B) · χ_λ(w)`\nwhere c_λ(B) are the expansion coefficients (which depend on B).\n\nFor this to be PD in w, we need c_λ(B) ≥ 0 for all λ. Is this the case?\n\nActually, `exp(β·Re Tr(B · w))` is NOT necessarily PD in w. The PD property of the plaquette factor is about the FULL plaquette variable U_p = w · A, not about the individual link w.\n\nHmm, so integrating out the temporal interface links is not straightforward.\n\nLet me think about this differently. Maybe the approach should be to use the character expansion of the plaquette factor.\n\nThe plaquette factor `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` has the character expansion:\n`exp(β·Re Tr(U_p)) = ∑_λ a_λ · χ_λ(U_p)` with a_λ ≥ 0.\n\nFor the upper interface plaquette at x:\n`exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · A_x)) = ∑_λ a_λ · χ_λ(w(x) · A_x)`\n\nwhere A_x = U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)† is the product of the other 3 links.\n\nNow, `χ_λ(w(x) · A_x) = Tr(ρ_λ(w(x) · A_x)) = Tr(ρ_λ(w(x)) · ρ_λ(A_x)) = ∑_{i,j} (ρ_λ(w(x)))_{ij} · (ρ_λ(A_x))_{ji}`.\n\nSo the plaquette factor is:\n`∑_λ a_λ · ∑_{i,j} (ρ_λ(w(x)))_{ij} · (ρ_λ(A_x))_{ji}`\n\nThis is a sum of matrix elements of w(x), weighted by the CG coefficients. The key: the matrix elements `(ρ_λ(w(x)))_{ij}` are the Peter-Weyl basis functions of w(x).\n\nNow, the reflection positivity integral involves f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · [plaquette factors]. The plaquette factors include the upper interface plaquettes (involving w) and the lower interface plaquettes (involving negative temporal links, not w).\n\nThe key: the temporal interface links w appear in f and in the upper interface plaquette factors. The reflected w⁻¹ appears in f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹).\n\nNow, if we expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of w:\n`f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_{λ,i,j} c_{λ,i,j}(U⁺, u⁰_s) · (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}`\n\nAnd `f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_{μ,k,l} c_{μ,k,l}(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · (ρ_μ(w⁻¹))_{kl}`\n\nNow, `(ρ_μ(w⁻¹))_{kl} = (ρ_μ(w)†)_{kl} = conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})` (because ρ_μ is unitary, so ρ_μ(w⁻¹) = ρ_μ(w)†).\n\nSo `f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_{μ,k,l} c_{μ,k,l}(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})`.\n\nAnd the upper interface plaquette factor (expanded in characters of w):\n`∑_λ a_λ · ∑_{i,j} (ρ_λ(w(x)))_{ij} · (ρ_λ(A_x))_{ji}`\n\nNow, the integral over w(x) of the product of three matrix elements:\n`∫ (ρ_λ(w))_{ij} · conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk}) · (ρ_ν(w))_{pq} dw`\n\nThis is a triple product integral, which is handled by the Clebsch-Gordan coefficients. This is exactly the `triple_product_character_matrix_integral` lemma that was proven!\n\nSo the approach IS the L² expansion, but applied to the TEMPORAL INTERFACE LINKS specifically. The key insight is:\n\n**Expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of the temporal interface links w. The reflection maps w → w⁻¹, which conjugates the matrix elements. The upper interface plaquette factor provides the third matrix element. The integral over w is a triple product integral, handled by the CG coefficients. The result is a Gram matrix (PSD), giving non-negativity.**\n\nThis is different from the L² expansion approach in §8.11.30-31, which expanded in the basis of ALL interface links. The key difference is:\n\n**Expand ONLY in the temporal interface links (which are inverted by the reflection), NOT in the spatial interface links (which are fixed by the reflection).**\n\nThe spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection, so they DON'T need to be expanded — they appear the same in both f-factors. Only the temporal interface links (which are inverted) need the Peter-Weyl expansion.\n\nThis is a crucial insight! The previous approach (§8.11.30-31) expanded in ALL interface links, which led to the reorganization obstacle. By expanding ONLY in the temporal interface links, the reorganization might be simpler.\n\nLet me think about why this works. The reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_W) dμ`\n\nThe Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·S_W)` is a product of:\n1. Positive plaquettes: depend on U⁺ (and possibly u⁰_s, w through the upper interface plaquettes)\n2. Negative plaquettes: depend on U⁻ (and possibly u⁰_s through the lower interface plaquettes)\n3. Interface plaquettes: S_spatial_0(u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) + S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰_s)\n\nThe key: w appears ONLY in f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w), f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹), and S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w).\n\nSo the integral over w is:\n`∫ f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) · [rest, independent of w] dw`\n\nThe \"rest\" is independent of w, so it can be pulled out. The integral over w is:\n`J(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) dw`\n\nNow, expand f and the plaquette factor in the Peter-Weyl basis of w:\n`f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_{λ,i,j} c_{λ,i,j}(U⁺, u⁰_s) · (ρ_λ(w))_{ij}`\n`f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_{μ,k,l} c_{μ,k,l}(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · conj((ρ_μ(w))_{lk})`\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∏_x [∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(w_x))_{pq} · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}]`\n\nWait, S_ts_upper is a SUM of plaquettes, so exp(-β·S_ts_upper) is a PRODUCT of plaquette factors. Each plaquette factor involves w(x) for a specific x. So the product is over x, and each factor is a function of w(x) only.\n\nSo the integral over w = (w(x₁), w(x₂), ...) factorizes as a product of integrals over each w(x):\n`J = ∏_x ∫ f_x(w(x)) · g_x(w(x)⁻¹) · K_x(w(x)) dw(x)`\n\nWait, no. f depends on ALL the w(x)'s (it's a function of the full w config), not just one w(x). So the integral doesn't factorize directly.\n\nBut if we expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of the FULL w config (tensor product of individual w(x)'s), then the integral factorizes.\n\nLet me think about this. f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) is a function of w = (w(x₁), w(x₂), ..., w(xₙ)) where x₁, ..., xₙ are the spatial sites. We can expand:\n`f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) = ∑_α c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · Φ_α(w)`\n\nwhere Φ_α(w) = ∏_x (ρ_{λ_x}(w(x)))_{i_x, j_x} are the Peter-Weyl basis functions of the product group (SU(N)^n).\n\nAnd `f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) = ∑_β c_β(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · conj(Φ_β(w))` (because (ρ_λ(w⁻¹))_{ij} = conj((ρ_λ(w))_{ji}), so the basis functions of w⁻¹ are the conjugates of the basis functions of w, with indices swapped).\n\nAnd `exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∏_x K_x(w(x))` where K_x is the plaquette factor at x.\n\nNow, the integral:\n`J = ∫ [∑_α c_α · Φ_α(w)] · [∑_β c_β' · conj(Φ_β(w))] · ∏_x K_x(w(x)) dw`\n\n`= ∑_{α,β} c_α · c_β' · ∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ_β(w)) · ∏_x K_x(w(x)) dw`\n\nNow, `Φ_α(w) = ∏_x (ρ_{λ_x}(w(x)))_{i_x, j_x}` and `conj(Φ_β(w)) = ∏_x conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w(x)))_{k_x, l_x})`.\n\nAnd `∏_x K_x(w(x)) = ∏_x [∑_ν a_ν · χ_ν(w(x))]` (the plaquette factor is a class function, so it's a sum of characters).\n\nWait, actually, `K_x(w(x)) = exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · A_x))` which is NOT a class function of w(x) (because A_x ≠ 1). So the character expansion is:\n`K_x(w(x)) = ∑_ν ∑_{p,q} d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) · (ρ_ν(w(x)))_{pq}`\n\nwhere d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) are the expansion coefficients (which depend on A_x, i.e., on the positive and spatial interface links).\n\nHmm, so K_x is NOT a class function of w(x). It's a general function, expanded in the matrix elements of w(x).\n\nNow, the integral:\n`∫ Φ_α(w) · conj(Φ_β(w)) · ∏_x K_x(w(x)) dw`\n\n`= ∫ ∏_x (ρ_{λ_x}(w(x)))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w(x)))_{k_x, l_x}) · [∑_ν ∑_{p,q} d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) · (ρ_ν(w(x)))_{pq}] dw`\n\n`= ∏_x ∫ (ρ_{λ_x}(w))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w))_{k_x, l_x}) · [∑_ν ∑_{p,q} d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) · (ρ_ν(w))_{pq}] dw`\n\n`= ∏_x ∑_ν ∑_{p,q} d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) · ∫ (ρ_{λ_x}(w))_{i_x, j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x}(w))_{k_x, l_x}) · (ρ_ν(w))_{pq} dw`\n\nEach factor is a TRIPLE PRODUCT integral of matrix elements, which is handled by the CG coefficients. This is exactly `triple_product_character_matrix_integral`!\n\nAnd the key: the triple product integral gives a Gram matrix structure (PSD), because the CG coefficients form a Gram matrix.\n\nSo the integral J is:\n`J = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β'(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · [product of triple product integrals]`\n\nAnd the triple product integrals give a Gram matrix structure, so J can be written as:\n`J = ∑_γ |∑_α P_{γ,α} · c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s)|² · [something involving c_β']`\n\nWait, I need to be more careful. The triple product integral `∫ (ρ_λ)_{ij} · conj((ρ_μ)_{kl}) · (ρ_ν)_{pq} dw` gives a sum over the intermediate representation σ:\n`∑_σ CG(σ; λ, μ, ν) · [Gram matrix in σ]`\n\nAnd the Gram matrix is PSD, so the overall sum is ≥ 0.\n\nBut the key question is: does the sum over α, β with the coefficients c_α and c_β' give a non-negative result?\n\nThe coefficients c_α depend on U⁺ (positive links) and c_β' depends on reflect(U⁻) (reflected negative links). These are DIFFERENT variables. So the sum is:\n`J = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺) · c_β'(reflect(U⁻)) · M_{α,β}`\n\nwhere M_{α,β} is the matrix of triple product integrals (which has Gram matrix structure).\n\nFor J to be ≥ 0, we need M to be PSD AND the sum to be a quadratic form. But c_α and c_β' are at DIFFERENT variables (U⁺ vs reflect(U⁻)), so this is NOT a standard quadratic form `x†·M·x`.\n\nHmm, so even with the temporal interface link expansion, we still face the issue that the two sets of coefficients are at different variables.\n\nBUT — the key insight is: after integrating out w, the result J(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) is a function of U⁺, U⁻, and u⁰_s. And then we need to integrate J over U⁺, U⁻, and u⁰_s (with the remaining Boltzmann factors).\n\nThe remaining Boltzmann factors include the positive plaquettes (depending on U⁺) and the negative plaquettes (depending on U⁻). These are PD functions of U⁺ and U⁻ respectively.\n\nSo the full integral is:\n`I = ∫ J(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·(S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s) + S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nwhere S_pos is the purely positive plaquette action, S_neg is the purely negative plaquette action, and S_spatial_0 is the pure spatial interface plaquette action.\n\nNow, J has the form:\n`J(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β'(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · M_{α,β}(u⁰_s)`\n\nwhere M_{α,β} is the Gram matrix from the triple product integrals (depending on u⁰_s and the positive links through A_x).\n\nWait, actually, M depends on A_x, which depends on U⁺ (positive links) and u⁰_s (spatial interface links). So M is NOT just a function of u⁰_s — it also depends on U⁺.\n\nThis complicates things. The matrix M depends on U⁺, so we can't simply pull it out of the U⁺ integral.\n\nHmm. Let me think about this more carefully.\n\nActually, the plaquette factor K_x(w(x)) = exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · A_x)) where A_x depends on U⁺ and u⁰_s. The expansion coefficients d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) depend on A_x, hence on U⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nSo the triple product integral at site x is:\n`∑_ν ∑_{p,q} d_{ν,p,q}(A_x) · ∫ (ρ_{λ_x})_{i_x,j_x} · conj((ρ_{μ_x})_{k_x,l_x}) · (ρ_ν)_{pq} dw`\n\nThis depends on A_x (hence on U⁺ and u⁰_s), so the Gram matrix M depends on U⁺.\n\nThis means J is NOT separable in U⁺ and U⁻, and the integral over U⁺ and U⁻ doesn't simplify.\n\nOK, I think this approach also has difficulties. The fundamental issue is that the plaquette factor couples the temporal interface links w to the positive links U⁺, so the expansion coefficients depend on U⁺.\n\nLet me think about whether there's a way around this.\n\nActually, maybe the key is to expand the plaquette factor K_x(w(x)) in characters of w(x) FIRST, and then use the PD property.\n\n`K_x(w(x)) = exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · A_x)) = ∑_ν a_ν · χ_ν(w(x) · A_x)`\n\nWait, but `χ_ν(w · A)` is NOT a character of w — it's a character of the product w·A. The character expansion of `exp(β·Re Tr(U_p))` is in terms of `χ_ν(U_p)` where U_p = w·A is the plaquette variable.\n\nSo `K_x = ∑_ν a_ν · χ_ν(w(x) · A_x) = ∑_ν a_ν · Tr(ρ_ν(w(x) · A_x)) = ∑_ν a_ν · Tr(ρ_ν(w(x)) · ρ_ν(A_x))`\n`= ∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{i,j} (ρ_ν(w(x)))_{ij} · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{ji}`\n\nSo the expansion coefficients are `a_ν · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{ji}`, which depend on A_x (hence on U⁺ and u⁰_s).\n\nNow, the integral over w(x) of the triple product:\n`∫ (ρ_λ)_{ij} · conj((ρ_μ)_{kl}) · [∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp} · (ρ_ν(w))_{pq}] dw`\n\n`= ∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp} · ∫ (ρ_λ)_{ij} · conj((ρ_μ)_{kl}) · (ρ_ν)_{pq} dw`\n\nThe triple product integral `∫ (ρ_λ)_{ij} · conj((ρ_μ)_{kl}) · (ρ_ν)_{pq} dw` is a CG coefficient (independent of A_x).\n\nSo the result is:\n`∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp} · CG(λ,i,j; μ,k,l; ν,p,q)`\n\nThis depends on A_x through `(ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}`.\n\nNow, the full integral J is:\n`J = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β'(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · ∏_x [∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp} · CG_x(α, β, ν, p, q)]`\n\nwhere A_x depends on U⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nThis is a complicated expression. The key question is whether the sum over α, β (with the CG structure) gives a non-negative result when integrated over U⁺ and U⁻.\n\nI think the key insight is that the CG coefficients have a specific structure that allows the sum to be reorganized as a sum of squares. But this requires the \"reorganization\" step that was identified as the key obstacle in §8.11.30-31.\n\nHowever, the reorganization might be SIMPLER in this case because:\n1. We're only expanding in the temporal interface links (not all interface links).\n2. The spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection (no expansion needed).\n3. The positive and negative links are separate variables.\n\nLet me think about whether the reorganization is simpler.\n\nActually, I think the key insight is:\n\n**After expanding in the temporal interface links and integrating them out, the result is a KERNEL K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) that is PD in (U⁺, U⁻) for fixed u⁰_s.**\n\nIf K is PD in (U⁺, U⁻), then:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · conj(h(U⁻)) · K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nAnd our integral is:\n`I = ∫∫ f̃(U⁺, u⁰_s) · f̃(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) · [remaining factors] dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nIf the remaining factors (positive and negative plaquette actions) can be absorbed into f̃, and if K is PD, then the integral is a PD quadratic form and is ≥ 0.\n\nBut the remaining factors `exp(-β·S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s))` and `exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s))` depend on U⁺ and U⁻ separately. If we absorb them:\n`h(U⁺, u⁰_s) = f(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s)/2)`\n`h'(U⁻, u⁰_s) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s)/2)`\n\nWait, but `f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)` — f is evaluated at reflect(U⁻), not U⁻. And `exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s))` is at U⁻. So:\n`h'(U⁻, u⁰_s) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s)/2)`\n\nBy reflection symmetry, `S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s) = S_pos(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)`. So:\n`h'(U⁻, u⁰_s) = f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)/2) = h(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)`\n\nSo `h'(U⁻, u⁰_s) = h(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)`.\n\nNow, the integral is:\n`I = ∫∫ h(U⁺, u⁰_s) · h(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nFor this to be ≥ 0, we need K to be PD in (U⁺, U⁻) AND the integral to be a PD quadratic form.\n\nThe PD quadratic form would be:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · conj(h(U⁻)) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0`\n\nBut our integral has `h(U⁺) · h(reflect(U⁻))`, NOT `h(U⁺) · conj(h(U⁻))`. And h is real-valued (since f is real and the Boltzmann factor is real), so conj(h) = h. But `h(reflect(U⁻))` is NOT `h(U⁻)` — it's h evaluated at the reflected config.\n\nSo the integral is:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(reflect(U⁻)) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻`\n\nFor this to be ≥ 0, we need K to satisfy:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(reflect(U⁻)) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nThis is NOT the standard PD condition. The standard PD condition is:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(U⁻) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nThe difference is `h(reflect(U⁻))` vs `h(U⁻)`. If we substitute V = reflect(U⁻) (measure-preserving):\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(V) · K(U⁺, reflect(V)) dμ⁺ dμV`\n\nSo the condition becomes:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(V) · K(U⁺, reflect(V)) dμ⁺ dμV ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nThis is the standard PD condition for the kernel `K'(U⁺, V) = K(U⁺, reflect(V))`. So we need `K'(U⁺, V) = K(U⁺, reflect(V))` to be PD.\n\nNow, is `K'(U⁺, V) = K(U⁺, reflect(V))` PD? If K is PD, is K' PD?\n\nK is PD means: `∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(U⁻) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\nK' is PD means: `∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(V) · K(U⁺, reflect(V)) dμ⁺ dμV ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nSubstituting V = reflect(U⁻) in the K' condition:\n`∫∫ h(U⁺) · h(reflect(U⁻)) · K(U⁺, U⁻) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ ≥ 0` for all h.\n\nThis is NOT the same as K being PD (which has `h(U⁺) · h(U⁻)`, not `h(U⁺) · h(reflect(U⁻))`).\n\nSo K' being PD is a DIFFERENT condition from K being PD. K being PD does NOT imply K' being PD.\n\nHmm, so even if K is PD, we can't conclude the integral is ≥ 0.\n\nBUT — maybe K has a special structure that makes K' PD. Specifically, if K(U⁺, U⁻) = K'(U⁺, reflect(U⁻)) (i.e., K is \"reflection-symmetric\"), then K' = K and K' is PD iff K is PD.\n\nIs K reflection-symmetric? K is the kernel obtained by integrating out the temporal interface links. By the reflection symmetry of the full Boltzmann factor, K should be reflection-symmetric: `K(U⁺, U⁻) = K(reflect(U⁻), reflect(U⁺))`... hmm, that's not quite what we need.\n\nActually, let me think about what K is. K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) is the result of integrating out w:\n`K(U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s) = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) · exp(-β·S_ts_lower(U⁻, u⁰_s)) dw`\n\nWait, no. K should be the kernel WITHOUT f. Let me reconsider.\n\nActually, I think I've been conflating two things. Let me separate:\n1. The kernel K (from the Boltzmann factor, after integrating out w).\n2. The function f (which depends on w).\n\nThe reflection positivity integral is:\n`I = ∫ f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) · f(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s, w⁻¹) · exp(-β·S_W) dμ`\n\nThe Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·S_W)` includes the temporal interface plaquette factors (which depend on w). If we expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of w and use the triple product integral, we get a kernel that depends on U⁺, U⁻, u⁰_s.\n\nBut the kernel is NOT just the Boltzmann factor with w integrated out — it's the result of the triple product integral, which involves the CG coefficients and the plaquette factor expansion.\n\nI think the key question is whether this kernel is PD in (U⁺, U⁻) for fixed u⁰_s, AND whether the \"reflection twist\" (h(reflect(U⁻)) vs h(U⁻)) can be handled.\n\nOK, I think I've spent enough time on analysis. I'm at 81% context. Let me summarize the key findings, document them, and prepare for handoff.\n\nThe key insight from this session is:\n\n**The temporal interface links are inverted by the reflection (w → w⁻¹), while the spatial interface links are fixed (u⁰_s → u⁰_s). This means the L² expansion only needs to be done in the temporal interface links, not all interface links. The triple product integral (already proven) handles the integration over w, giving a Gram matrix structure. The remaining question is whether the resulting kernel is PD in (U⁺, U⁻) with the \"reflection twist\" h(reflect(U⁻)) vs h(U⁻).**\n\n**The reflection twist can be handled if the kernel K satisfies K(U⁺, U⁻) = K(reflect(U⁻), reflect(U⁺)) (reflection-symmetric), which would make K'(U⁺, V) = K(U⁺, reflect(V)) = K(reflect(V), reflect(U⁺))... hmm, this needs more analysis.**\n\nActually, let me think about this one more time. The key is:\n\nAfter expanding f in the Peter-Weyl basis of w and integrating out w using the triple product integral, we get:\n`J = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · M_{α,β}(U⁺, u⁰_s)`\n\nwhere M is the Gram matrix from the triple product integrals.\n\nNow, M depends on U⁺ (through the plaquette factor expansion coefficients, which depend on A_x, which depends on U⁺). So M is NOT independent of U⁺.\n\nBut the key: M has a Gram matrix structure. Specifically, from the triple product integral:\n`M_{α,β} = ∑_γ P_γ(α) · conj(P_γ(β)) · a_γ`\n\nwhere P_γ are the CG coefficients and a_γ ≥ 0 are the plaquette factor coefficients.\n\nWait, but the plaquette factor coefficients depend on A_x (hence on U⁺). So a_γ = a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s).\n\nHmm, so M_{α,β}(U⁺, u⁰_s) = ∑_γ P_γ(α) · conj(P_γ(β)) · a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s).\n\nAnd J = ∑_{α,β} c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · c_β(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · ∑_γ P_γ(α) · conj(P_γ(β)) · a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s).\n\n= ∑_γ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · [∑_α c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α)] · [∑_β c_β(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · conj(P_γ(β))]\n\n= ∑_γ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(D_γ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s))\n\nwhere D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) = ∑_α c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α).\n\nNow, a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) ≥ 0 (the plaquette factor coefficients are non-negative). And D_γ is a function of U⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nSo J = ∑_γ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(D_γ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)).\n\nNow, the full integral is:\n`I = ∫ J · exp(-β·(S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s) + S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s) + S_spatial_0(u⁰_s))) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\n`= ∫ ∑_γ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · conj(D_γ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·(S_pos + S_neg + S_spatial_0)) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nNow, a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) depends on U⁺, and it's related to the plaquette factor. In fact, a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) = a_γ · (ρ_γ(A_x))_{...} which involves the positive links.\n\nHmm, this is getting very complicated. The dependence of a_γ on U⁺ makes it hard to separate the integral.\n\nLet me think about whether a_γ can be absorbed into D_γ or the Boltzmann factor.\n\nActually, a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) comes from the expansion of the plaquette factor K_x(w(x)) = exp(β·Re Tr(w(x)·A_x)). The expansion is:\n`K_x(w(x)) = ∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp} · (ρ_ν(w(x)))_{pq}`\n\nSo a_γ involves a_ν · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}, which depends on A_x (the product of the other 3 links in the plaquette, which are positive and spatial interface links).\n\nNow, A_x involves positive links (spatial at t=1, temporal at t=1) and spatial interface links. So a_γ depends on U⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nThe key: a_γ is NOT just a non-negative constant — it's a non-negative function of U⁺ and u⁰_s. And it's related to the POSITIVE plaquette factor.\n\nActually, I think a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) is part of the POSITIVE Boltzmann factor. The upper interface plaquette factor `exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w))` involves both w and U⁺. When we expand in w, the coefficients involve U⁺.\n\nSo the integral I has the form:\n`I = ∫ ∑_γ [a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s))] · [conj(D_γ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s))] · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\nLet me define:\n`E_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) = a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s))`\n`F_γ(U⁻, u⁰_s) = conj(D_γ(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_neg(U⁻, u⁰_s))`\n\nThen:\n`I = ∫ ∑_γ E_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · F_γ(U⁻, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) dμ⁺ dμ⁻ dμ⁰_s`\n\n`= ∫ exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∑_γ [∫ E_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁺] · [∫ F_γ(U⁻, u⁰_s) dμ⁻] dμ⁰_s`\n\nNow, by the change of variables U⁻ → reflect(V⁺) (measure-preserving):\n`∫ F_γ(U⁻, u⁰_s) dμ⁻ = ∫ conj(D_γ(reflect(reflect(V⁺)), u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_neg(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s)) dμ⁺`\n`= ∫ conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_pos(V⁺, u⁰_s)) dμ⁺`\n\n(using reflect(reflect(V⁺)) = V⁺ and S_neg(reflect(V⁺), u⁰_s) = S_pos(V⁺, u⁰_s))\n\nAnd `∫ E_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁺ = ∫ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s)) dμ⁺`.\n\nSo:\n`I = ∫ exp(-β·S_spatial_0(u⁰_s)) · ∑_γ [∫ a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · D_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) · exp(-β·S_pos) dμ⁺] · [∫ conj(D_γ(V⁺, u⁰_s)) · exp(-β·S_pos) dμ⁺] dμ⁰_s`\n\nNow, if a_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) is a REAL NON-NEGATIVE function, then:\n`∫ a_γ · D_γ · exp(-β·S_pos) dμ⁺` and `∫ conj(D_γ) · exp(-β·S_pos) dμ⁺` are NOT conjugates of each other (because a_γ is in the first but not the second).\n\nSo the sum is:\n`∑_γ [∫ a_γ · D_γ · exp(-β·S_pos)] · [∫ conj(D_γ) · exp(-β·S_pos)]`\n\nThis is NOT `∑_γ |∫ D_γ · exp(-β·S_pos)|²` because a_γ is in the first factor but not the second.\n\nHmm. So the non-negativity is NOT obvious.\n\nBUT — if a_γ is a constant (independent of U⁺), then:\n`∑_γ a_γ · [∫ D_γ · exp(-β·S_pos)] · [∫ conj(D_γ) · exp(-β·S_pos)] = ∑_γ a_γ · |∫ D_γ · exp(-β·S_pos)|² ≥ 0`\n\nSo the key question is: **is a_γ independent of U⁺?**\n\na_γ comes from the expansion of the upper interface plaquette factor, which involves A_x (the product of the other 3 links). A_x depends on U⁺, so a_γ depends on U⁺.\n\nUNLESS we can absorb the U⁺-dependence of a_γ into D_γ or the Boltzmann factor.\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The upper interface plaquette factor is:\n`exp(-β·S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)) = ∏_x exp(β·Re Tr(w(x) · A_x(U⁺, u⁰_s)))`\n\nwhere A_x = U((1,x), i) · U((1,x+î), 0)† · U((0,x), i)† depends on U⁺ and u⁰_s.\n\nNow, A_x is a product of 3 links: 2 positive links and 1 spatial interface link. The key: A_x is a function of U⁺ and u⁰_s, NOT of U⁻ or w.\n\nWhen we expand `exp(β·Re Tr(w · A_x))` in the Peter-Weyl basis of w:\n`exp(β·Re Tr(w · A_x)) = ∑_ν a_ν · χ_ν(w · A_x) = ∑_ν a_ν · Tr(ρ_ν(w) · ρ_ν(A_x))`\n`= ∑_ν a_ν · ∑_{p,q} (ρ_ν(w))_{pq} · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}`\n\nSo the coefficient of `(ρ_ν(w))_{pq}` is `a_ν · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}`, which depends on A_x (hence on U⁺ and u⁰_s).\n\nNow, the key insight: `a_ν · (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}` is a MATRIX ELEMENT of A_x, scaled by a_ν. And A_x is a product of positive and spatial interface links.\n\nSo the U⁺-dependence of a_γ is through the matrix elements `(ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}`, which are functions of U⁺.\n\nNow, can we absorb this into D_γ? Let me think...\n\nD_γ(U⁺, u⁰_s) = ∑_α c_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · P_γ(α), where c_α are the Peter-Weyl coefficients of f(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) in w, and P_γ are the CG coefficients.\n\nThe c_α depend on U⁺ and u⁰_s (they're the coefficients of f when expanded in w, for fixed U⁺ and u⁰_s).\n\nSo D_γ depends on U⁺ through c_α. And a_γ depends on U⁺ through (ρ_ν(A_x))_{qp}.\n\nThe product a_γ · D_γ involves both dependencies. Can we combine them?\n\nActually, I think the key is that a_γ · D_γ can be written as a SINGLE function of U⁺ and u⁰_s, and this function is related to the positive Boltzmann factor.\n\nLet me think about this differently. The full positive Boltzmann factor (including the upper interface plaquettes) is:\n`exp(-β·(S_pos(U⁺, u⁰_s) + S_ts_upper(U⁺, u⁰_s, w)))`\n\nThis is a function of U⁺, u⁰_s, and w. When we expand in w and integrate out w (using the triple product), the result involves the positive Boltzmann factor with w integrated out.\n\nThe key: the positive Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·(S_pos + S_ts_upper))` is a PD function of the POSITIVE links U⁺ (for fixed u⁰_s and w). This is because it's a product of plaquette factors, each of which is PD.\n\nWait, is the positive Boltzmann factor PD in U⁺? The positive plaquettes are entirely in the positive half, and each plaquette factor is PD. The product of PD functions is PD (by the Schur product theorem for class functions, or more generally for PD functions on groups).\n\nHmm, but the upper interface plaquettes involve BOTH U⁺ and w. So the positive Boltzmann factor `exp(-β·(S_pos + S_ts_upper))` is a function of both U⁺ and w, and it's PD in the plaquette variables (which are products of links), not necessarily in U⁺ alone.\n\nOK, I think I'm going in circles. Let me take a completely different approach and think about what's the simplest possible path to closing the axiom.\n\nActually, let me reconsider the problem from scratch. The key identity is:\n`∫ G·G(θU) dμ₀ = ∫ g·Tg dμ⁺⁰`\n\nAnd we need `∫ g·Tg ≥ 0`.\n\nWhat if we could prove T is positive directly, by showing T = A†·A for some operator A?\n\nIn the Lüscher approach, T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} where V is positive (multiplication) and U is positive (PD kernel). Then T = (U^{1/2}·V^{1/2})†·(U^{1/2}·V^{1/2}) = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} (since U and V are self-adjoint). Wait, that's T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} = (U^{1/2}·V^{1/2})†·(U^{1/2}·V^{1/2}) only if U^{1/2} and V^{1/2} commute, which they don't in general.\n\nActually, T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} is positive because for any f:\n`⟨f, Tf⟩ = ⟨f, V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}·f⟩ = ⟨V^{1/2}f, U·V^{1/2}f⟩ ≥ 0`\nbecause U is positive. This doesn't require U and V to commute.\n\nSo the key is: T = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} where V is a positive multiplication operator and U is a positive integral operator.\n\nNow, can we write our transfer matrix in this form?\n\nOur transfer matrix is:\n`(Tg)(u) = exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2) · ∫ g(reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)) · exp(-β·(S⁻(U⁻,u)/2 + S_int(U⁻,u))) dμ⁻`\n\nThe `exp(-β·S⁺(u)/2)` factor is a multiplication operator (V^{1/2}). The integral is the \"hopping\" part.\n\nBut the hopping part is NOT a standard integral operator because the argument of g is `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)`, which depends on BOTH U⁻ and u⁰.\n\nIn the Lüscher approach, the hopping operator is:\n`(Uf)(U_spatial) = ∫ K(U_spatial, V_spatial) f(V_spatial) dV_spatial`\n\nwhere the argument of f is V_spatial (independent of U_spatial). This is a standard integral operator.\n\nIn our case, the argument of g is `reflectToPosInterface(U⁻, u⁰)`, which has positive links = reflect(U⁻) and interface links = θ(u⁰). The interface links θ(u⁰) depend on u (specifically u⁰), so the argument of g depends on BOTH U⁻ and u.\n\nThis is NOT a standard integral operator. The \"hopping\" part is not a standard PD kernel operator.\n\nThe issue is the temporal interface links: θ(u⁰) ≠ u⁰ because the temporal links are inverted.\n\nIf the interface consisted only of spatial links (which are fixed by the reflection), then θ(u⁰) = u⁰, and the hopping part would be a standard integral operator:\n`(Ug)(u⁺, u⁰_s) = ∫ g(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · K(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁻`\n\nwhere the argument of g is (reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s), which has the SAME interface links u⁰_s as u. This IS a standard integral operator (in the positive links, with u⁰_s as a parameter).\n\nSo the key obstacle is the TEMPORAL INTERFACE LINKS. If we could eliminate them (or handle them separately), the transfer matrix would be a standard integral operator, and the Lüscher approach would work.\n\n**This is the key insight: the temporal interface links are the obstacle. If we integrate them out first (using the character expansion and triple product integral), the remaining transfer matrix is a standard integral operator in the spatial links, and the Lüscher approach (V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) gives positivity.**\n\nSo the proof strategy is:\n1. **Integrate out the temporal interface links w** using the character expansion and triple product integral. This gives a new transfer matrix T' that acts on functions of spatial links only.\n2. **Show T' = V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}** where V is the spatial plaquette factor (positive multiplication) and U is the temporal plaquette operator (PD kernel in spatial links).\n3. **Conclude T' is positive**, hence `∫ g·T'g ≥ 0`, hence `∫ g·Tg ≥ 0`.\n\nStep 1 is the L² expansion in the temporal interface links, using the triple product integral (already proven). Step 2 is the Lüscher decomposition. Step 3 is standard operator theory.\n\nThis is a clear path forward! The key difference from the previous approach (§8.11.30-31) is:\n- Previous approach: expand in ALL interface links → reorganization obstacle.\n- New approach: expand ONLY in temporal interface links → standard integral operator → Lüscher decomposition.\n\nThe reorganization obstacle is avoided because:\n1. The spatial interface links are fixed by the reflection (no expansion needed).\n2. After integrating out the temporal interface links, the remaining operator is a standard integral operator (PD kernel in spatial links).\n3. The Lüscher decomposition V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2} gives positivity directly, without needing a reorganization.\n\nThis is a significant insight! Let me document it and prepare for handoff.\n\nBut wait, I need to verify that step 1 actually works. The key question is: after integrating out w, is the resulting operator T' a standard integral operator with a PD kernel?\n\nLet me think about this. After integrating out w, the transfer matrix becomes:\n`(T'g')(u⁺, u⁰_s) = ∫ g'(reflect(U⁻), u⁰_s) · K'(U⁻, u⁺, u⁰_s) dμ⁻`\n\nwhere g' is a function of spatial links only (positive + spatial interface), and K' is the kernel obtained by integrating out w.\n\nWait, but g originally depends on w (the temporal interface links). After integrating out w, g is replaced by its Peter-Weyl expansion, and the result is a sum over representations. Each term in the sum is a function of spatial links only, multiplied by a CG coefficient.\n\nSo T' is not a single integral operator but a SUM of integral operators (one for each representation in the expansion of g in w).\n\nHmm, this complicates things. Let me think more carefully.\n\nActually, I think the right way to think about it is:\n\nThe transfer matrix T acts on functions of (U⁺, u⁰_s, w). We decompose the Hilbert space as:\n`L²(U⁺, u⁰_s, w) = L²(U⁺, u⁰_s) ⊗ L²(w)`\n\nAnd the transfer matrix T acts on this tensor product. The key: T can be written as:\n`T = ∑_γ T_γ ⊗ M_γ`\n\nwhere T_γ acts on L²(U⁺, u⁰_s) and M_γ acts on L²(w).\n\nThe M_γ are related to the plaquette factor expansion (they're the \"matrix element\" operators of the plaquette factor in the w basis).\n\nThe key: each T_γ is a standard integral operator (in the spatial links), and the M_γ provide the \"weight\" (from the CG coefficients and plaquette factor).\n\nFor T to be positive, we need:\n`⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∑_γ ⟨g_spatial, T_γ g_spatial⟩ · ⟨g_w, M_γ g_w⟩ ≥ 0`\n\nwhere g = g_spatial ⊗ g_w (in the tensor product decomposition).\n\nBut g is NOT a pure tensor product — it's a general function of (U⁺, u⁰_s, w). So the decomposition is:\n`g = ∑_α g_α(U⁺, u⁰_s) · φ_α(w)`\n\nwhere φ_α are the Peter-Weyl basis functions of w.\n\nAnd:\n`⟨g, Tg⟩ = ∑_{α,β} ⟨g_α, T_{α,β} g_β⟩ · [integral over w of φ_α · φ_β · plaquette factor]`\n\nwhere T_{α,β} are the \"matrix elements\" of T in the w basis.\n\nThis is getting complicated. The key question is whether the matrix T_{α,β} (with the w-integral weights) forms a PSD matrix.\n\nI think the answer is yes, because of the Gram matrix structure of the triple product integral. But the details are involved.\n\nOK, I think I've done enough analysis. Let me document the key insight and prepare for handoff. The key insight is:\n\n**The temporal interface links are the obstacle. By expanding ONLY in the temporal interface links (not all interface links) and using the triple product integral (already proven), the transfer matrix can be decomposed into a sum of standard integral operators in the spatial links, weighted by Gram matrix elements (PSD). The Lüscher decomposition (V^{1/2}·U·V^{1/2}) then gives positivity for each term.**\n\nThis is a clear path forward that avoids the reorganization obstacle of the previous approach. The next session should:\n1. Formalize the decomposition of the interface into spatial and temporal links.\n2. Expand f in the Peter-Weyl basis of the temporal interface links.\n3. Use the triple product integral to integrate out the temporal interface links.\n4. Show the resulting operator is a sum of PD kernel operators (Lüscher decomposition).\n5. Conclude positivity.\n\nLet me document this in the design doc and update the task lists.\n\nActually, I realize I should also check whether the build is still green, since I haven't made any code changes.\n\nLet me write the design doc update, update the task lists, and prepare for handoff. I'm at 81% context, so I should handoff soon.\n\nLet me first append the analysis to the design doc.","turn":24,"is_prune_marker":false}
